## Startup roots and repository binding

Configuration and board storage are independent inputs. `TASK_BOARD_CONFIG`
selects an explicit config file; otherwise task-board reads
`task-board.config.json` from the invocation project root. `TASK_BOARD_DIR`
selects only local board Store/Resources and never changes config discovery,
runtime state, or Git authority. An explicit config is a frozen regular-file
identity: it must exist at startup, its canonical path/root are persisted, and
deletion or movement before runner launch refuses instead of silently using an
empty/default config.

Managed spawn freezes three explicit non-board roots: project root (roles and
default config), runtime root (prompts, logwork, RUN state/events/directives and
scheduler state), and control root (the Git repository owning worktree and
Change Request authority). The launcher propagates them to children as
`TASK_BOARD_PROJECT_ROOT`, `TASK_BOARD_RUNTIME_ROOT`, and
`TASK_BOARD_CONTROL_ROOT`. Colocated projects keep the prior layout, but
`dirname(TASK_BOARD_DIR)` is never an authority fallback. Repository-aware and
read-only commands resolve the same project/runtime identity, including from a
nested invocation; requiring Git adds binding validation without moving RUN or
store state.

The control repository must expose exactly one canonical fetch binding. The
frozen tuple is `remote_name`, `canonical_remote_url`,
`source=runtime_control_repository_binding`, and `binding_sha256`; children
inherit its `TASK_BOARD_REPOSITORY_*` representation and run manifests persist
the tuple. Direct children and managed Codex/Claude Session Manager envelopes
carry the complete project/config/runtime/control/binding identity. Managed
hosts apply it over the long-lived daemon environment and validate it against
the run before attach, so a stale daemon or another checkout cannot substitute
authority. This binding does not inspect the current branch, branch tracking,
remote HEAD, `origin`, or `integration_base_branch`. Zero or multiple remotes,
multiple canonical URLs, unreadable bindings, inherited name/URL/digest
disagreement, and missing/moved/foreign/ambiguous roots fail closed before any
board mutation, branch/worktree creation, prompt write, or RUN registration.
Protected-trunk authority resolution happens later and remains the separate
algorithm documented below.

A key declared twice inside the same object is refused as
`duplicate_sidecar_config_key`, naming the dotted key and the line and column of
both occurrences. JSON decoding resolves a duplicated member last-wins and
discards the earlier one silently, so the file an operator reads and the policy
task-board applies could otherwise disagree — a landing gate naming a real suite
followed by a second `commands` key with an empty list would resolve as
configured-empty and land. The scan covers the whole document at every depth,
root and `remote` profile alike. A key that repeats across DIFFERENT objects is
ordinary and stays legal: several ceilings blocks each carry `model`, and a
child object may reuse a name from its parent.

Unknown keys, wrong types, unsupported enum values, conflicting legacy/current
sections, and malformed containers are refused as a whole. Task-board does not
continue using understood siblings, guess defaults for rejected fields, or
offer an automatic fix. Found-value summaries are typed and bounded; secret,
token, password, credential, and key-shaped fields plus credential-like nested
values are redacted before diagnostics are stored or rendered.

## Root sections

| Key | Type | Required | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `mode` | `"local"` or `"remote"` | No | `"local"` | Selects the active project profile. |
| `local` | object or `null` | No | `{"board_dir":".task-board"}` | Local board location. |
| `remote` | object or `null` | For a stored remote profile | None | Shared remote server/board profile. |
| `spawn` | object or `null` | No | Runtime defaults | Root spawn policy; remote mode uses it when `remote.spawn` is absent/null. |
| `features` | object or `null` | No | Built-in feature defaults | Root feature registry; remote mode uses it when `remote.features` is absent/null. |
| `agent_context` | object or `null` | No; compatibility only | None | One-release legacy alternative to `features.agent_context`; never configure both. |
| `contexts` | object or `null` | No | No clearance, no allowlist, default retention | Context security and retention policy: role clearance for restricted snapshots, the context-builder read allowlist, and the retention/GC windows and caps. |
| `session_manager` | object or `null` | No | Built-in Codex continuation defaults | Current Session Manager provider settings. |
| `codex` | object or `null` | No; compatibility only | None | Legacy location for Codex session settings; `session_manager` takes precedence. |
| `version_control` | object or `null` | No | `confirm=false` | Scoped commit acknowledgement policy. |
| `validation` | object or `null` | No | Strict validation | Local-board validation compatibility policy. |

> `session_manager`'s "provider" is a different, binary axis (Codex or Claude as the
> Session Manager host) from the `spawn.runtimes`/`spawn.ceilings` runtime axis used
> everywhere else in this reference. It is separately owned (design task 14) rather
> than renamed by this pass.

## Local and remote profiles

| Key | Type | Required | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `local.board_dir` | string path | No | `".task-board"` | Board directory, resolved from the project. |
| `remote.url` | URL string | Yes for stored remote profile | None | Board server URL. |
| `remote.board` | non-empty string | Yes for stored remote profile | None | Remote board ID. |
| `remote.tls_no_verify` | boolean | No | `false` | Disables TLS certificate verification. |
| `remote.spawn` | spawn object or `null` | No | Root `spawn` | Remote-only spawn policy. |
| `remote.features` | features object or `null` | No | Root `features` | Remote-only feature registry. |
| `remote.agent_context` | agent-context object or `null` | No; compatibility only | Root/feature defaults | Legacy remote alternative to `remote.features.agent_context`; never configure both. |

`remote.user` and `remote.token` are not canonical shared-project keys. Select a
user with `task-board switch-user`; store tokens with `task-board login` or use
the explicit `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` environment override. A stored `remote.token`
refuses every command until it is removed manually. Diagnostics report only
that the credential is present and its shape/length; they never echo its value.
To retire a legacy `remote.user`, persist that exact user for the same normalized
`remote.url + remote.board` scope, preview `task-board migrate-project-config`,
and apply with the preview digest. A missing or different durable selection is a
manual-remediation blocker; migration never guesses, rolls back a second store,
or changes credentials while removing the alias. `login`, `switch-user`, and
`logout` mutate machine-local auth state only. `logout` refuses before deleting
a token while the matching project profile still contains `remote.user`.
While that alias exists, `login` and `switch-user` accept only the same user so
their success always matches effective resolution; selecting a different user
requires migrating the legacy alias first. Managed config mutations resolve an
existing symlink to one target for read, advisory lock, staging, and rename, so
the symlink is preserved and alternate path spellings serialize together. The
lock coordinates task-board writers; arbitrary external editors are outside
that advisory boundary and the reviewed digest must still be checked.

### Authority and remote-mode scope

Remote mode does not make the board server authoritative for the entire project
configuration. `remote.url`, `remote.board`, and TLS selection are client
connection data. Root `validation`, `session_manager`, legacy `codex`, and
`version_control` remain effective client/project inputs in remote mode and are
not missing parity. The same is true of `contexts.clearance`,
`contexts.builder_allowlist`, `spawn.limits`, runtime `home_env`, and worktree
validation commands: they are enforced by the local consumer and are never
uploaded as board policy. Existing `remote.spawn` remains the same shared
`SpawnConfig` compatibility shape, so these nested spawn keys are still parsed
there when already configured; that compatibility does not change their
machine/project authority.

The executable classification source is
`pkg/remoteconfig/authority_schema.go`. It records current authority, valid
scope, owner, missing/null behavior, sensitivity, and separately records any
proposed authority move. The reviewed v1 decisions reject moving context
sharing, either goal gate, payload consent, or retention to per-board server
policy. The board-server deployment value `contexts.payloads.mode` remains an
independent deployment-global ceiling. No per-board policy payload is defined by
this schema revision.

That empty backend-policy set is intentional. Remote-mode parity is owned by
the local client projection: the checked v4 fixtures cover the current shared
spawn/feature surface, root-only client policies stay at their documented
consumers, and `project_config()` reports provenance with a null server
capability. Board-server exposes no project-policy route, payload, storage, or
capability negotiation for authority schema v1.

### Validation

`validation.allow_legacy_empty_done_containers` is an optional boolean and
defaults to `false`. It affects local-board validation only. When enabled, it
suppresses only the historical mismatch for an empty Story/Epic already stored
as `done`; active containers and containers with children remain strict. The
section is intentionally root-only and continues to apply to local validation
operations even when the selected board profile is remote.

`validation.mirror_run_progress_rows` is an optional boolean and defaults to
`false`. By default the derived activity mirror carries a spawn run's state
transitions and durable facts only (`queued`, `slot_claimed`, `goal_*`,
directive, reselection and terminal rows); the run's `progress` rows — one per
child board mutation, phase change and validation-suite step — stay in the run
ledger under `.temp/spawn-runs/<RUN>/events.jsonl`, which `spawn events` and
`observe` read in full. Set it to `true` to mirror progress rows onto element
activity as well. The mirror writer and `task-board validate` read the same
key, so an unmirrored progress row is never reported as `MISSING_LEDGER_MIRROR`
unless the project opted in. Rows mirrored before the default changed are kept
(the activity stream is append-only); the key only changes what new runs write.

## Spawn policy

| Key | Type | Required | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `spawn.enabled` | boolean | No | `true` | Enables tracked child spawning. |
| `spawn.max_parallel` | positive integer | No | `20` | Per-client concurrent runner limit. |
| `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` | exactly one of `{"exclusive":"runtime"}` or `{"mixed":["runtime",...]}` | No | Runtime affinity | Allowed/preferred runtime set. |
| `spawn.preferred_agent` | runtime string | No; compatibility only | None | Legacy single-runtime preference; do not combine with `preferred_agentic_system`. |
| `spawn.schedule.windows` | non-empty array | No | No schedule | UTC runtime-policy windows. |
| `spawn.schedule.windows[].start_utc` | `"HH:MM"` | Yes in a window | None | Inclusive UTC start. |
| `spawn.schedule.windows[].end_utc` | `"HH:MM"` | Yes in a window | None | Exclusive UTC end; an earlier value crosses midnight. |
| `spawn.schedule.windows[].preferred_agentic_system` | tagged runtime union | Yes in a window | None | Replaces the base runtime set during the window. |
| `spawn.launch_composition.enabled` | boolean | No | `true` | Enables optional agents-infra MCP composition. |
| `spawn.validation.max_concurrent_suites` | integer `>= 1` | No | `2` | Machine-scoped FIFO cap shared by producer handoff validation and integration revalidation for the same OS account. |
| `spawn.worktree_isolation.integration_base_branch` | branch name string | No | Resolved (see below) | Trunk that Story branches fork from and land on. |
| `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` | array of shell command strings | Yes inside a present `validation` block | Absent block refuses `integrate` (see below) | Landing-gate suite run against the exact tree `worktree integrate` will land. |
| `spawn.worktree_isolation.board_repository.root` | path string | Yes inside a present `board_repository` block | Absent block = colocated | Main working tree of the repository that owns the board; makes `worktree complete` the delivery path (see below). |
| `spawn.worktree_isolation.board_repository.integration_base_branch` | branch name string | No | Resolved from the board repository | Pins the board repository's protected default. |
| `spawn.worktree_isolation.board_publication` | `push` \\| `commit_only` \\| `off` | No | `push` | What `close-landed` and colocated `integrate` do with the board state after the done transition (see below). |
| `spawn.worktree_isolation.run_write_boundary` | `"warn"` or `"refuse"` | No | `"warn"` | Run write boundary policy: `refuse` marks violating runs and blocks launch, verdicts and delivery until an operator clears them; `warn` reports the same findings loudly and completes (see below). |
| `spawn.ceilings.contract_version` | `"spawn-policy-v2"`, `"spawn-policy-v3"`, or `"spawn-policy-v4"` | No | `"spawn-policy-v2"` | Selects one ceiling schema. v3 and v4 share one wire shape (see [`spawn-policy-v3` / `spawn-policy-v4`](#spawn-policy-v3--spawn-policy-v4)); v4 is only the version new v2 migration guidance now targets. |
| `spawn.ceilings.<runtime>.supervision` | object | No | Unconfigured (see below) | Orchestrator supervision in minutes; accepted under both contract versions. |
| `spawn.workload_classes` | object keyed by workload class | No | Unconfigured | Advisory ordered model/effort catalogs; a present object requires a non-empty `unified` fallback. |
| `spawn.runtimes.<runtime_id>` | runtime declaration object | No | No declarations | Declares a non-built-in `(agentic system, broker)` binding plus optional public model/deployment provenance; see [Declaring runtimes](#declaring-runtimes). |
| `spawn.limits.backoff.<key>` | object keyed by broker (or, for one release, a legacy runtime ID) | No | Shipped ladder per broker | Per-broker suppression ladder; see [Rate-limit knobs](#rate-limit-knobs) for the broker re-key and the one-release legacy runtime-ID spelling. |

Every root `spawn.*` key may instead appear under `remote.spawn.*`.

The validation-suite cap is deliberately different from `spawn.max_parallel`:
the latter bounds child runners per control root, while
`spawn.validation.max_concurrent_suites` bounds heavyweight configured suites
across every board owned by the same OS account. A waiting run records
`validation_queued` with its FIFO position and current holder run IDs; after it
acquires a slot it records `validation_started`. Tickets, holders, and the
state-lock owner carry PID plus process-start identity; heartbeats let the next
sweep reclaim a dead waiter or holder without confusing PID reuse for
liveness, and a stale lock left by a crashed writer is reclaimed too. A
matching live process identity is retained even when its heartbeat is late.
Cancelling a queued run removes its ticket and never launches a suite command.
Non-integer, zero, and negative values are refused as
`invalid_spawn_config` at the exact dotted key.

### Workload-class recommendations

`spawn.workload_classes` is opt-in and advisory. Its accepted keys are
`architecture`, `implementation`, `mechanical`, `debugging`, `testing`,
`review`, `research`, `documentation`, `migration`, `operations`, and the
mandatory `unified` fallback. Each value is an ordered array of exact
`{"runtime","model","reasoning_effort"?}` entries. A registered model decides
whether `reasoning_effort` is required or forbidden and which values are legal;
retired model rows remain loadable and are reported as unavailable by preflight.
Unknown class/entry keys, duplicate triples, malformed entries, and a missing or
empty `unified` array fail closed with typed diagnostics.

The selected policy is the same whole `spawn` section used by ceilings. In
remote mode, a present non-null `remote.spawn` never inherits root
`spawn.workload_classes`; omission there means unconfigured. Inspect the raw
effective catalog with `project_config().spawn.workload_classes`. Resolve one
decision with:

```bash
task-board q --format compact \
  'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=developer, agent=codex, task_class=code)'
```

For cross-provider ranking, first omit `agent`:

```bash
task-board q --format compact \
  'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=analyst, workload_class=research)'
```

Then narrow with `agent=RUNTIME` and use that query's fresh snapshot for spawn.
`providers.allowed` reports runtimes allowed by the effective policy (including
an active schedule override); `providers.targeted` names
all runtimes targeted by this query (all allowed runtimes when agent-less).
The existing singular `providers.target` describes the resolved agent context,
not the cross-provider ranking scope. Entries allowed by policy but outside
`agent=RUNTIME` are dropped with `runtime_not_targeted_by_query`; policy-excluded
entries retain `provider_not_allowed_by_preferred_agentic_system`.

Use `workload_class=mechanical` instead of `task_class=...` for an intentional
override. The response returns deterministic `available_pairs`, the ordered
`recommendations` subset, filtering provenance, and a content-bound snapshot
digest. Recommendations never widen `preferred_agentic_system`, schedule,
role-ceiling, registry, or provider-limit authority and never select a model.
If a provider-limit state read is indeterminate, the response keeps class
provenance but withholds pairs, filtering, and snapshot entirely.

When this section is configured, agents must use `query -> reason -> explicit
spawn`: assess scope, complexity, risk, autonomy, validation burden, and
expected context/token cost; choose an explicit available pair; then pass the
same derivation transport (`--workload-task-class`) or explicit override
(`--workload-class`), the fresh `--recommendation-snapshot-digest`, and one
non-empty `--recommendation-rationale`. A non-ranked available pair is legal
when the rationale explains the deliberate rejection. Spawn never reads rank as
authority and never fills a missing model or effort from recommendations.

### Story worktree isolation

Repository-mutating producer spawns run in a Story-scoped Git worktree rather
than in the shared checkout. Delivery is trunk-based: each Story gets one
short-lived `task-board/story/<STORY-ID>` branch forked from trunk, and it lands
directly on trunk only when the complete Story candidate is review-accepted.

```json
{
  "spawn": {
    "worktree_isolation": {
      "integration_base_branch": "main",
      "validation": {
        "commands": ["make test", "make vet"]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

`integration_base_branch`, `validation`, `board_repository` and
`integration_base_branch`, `validation`, `board_repository`,
`board_publication` and `run_write_boundary` are the only keys this
file accepts under
`worktree_isolation`. **There is no `enabled` flag, and configuring one is
a config error rather than a silently ignored field.** Isolation is the
standard path, not a feature toggle: the delivery model forbids feature flags
and partial trunk landing, and a capability that can be switched off is a
feature flag whatever it is called. Granularity (`story`), history (`squash`),
and integration (`ff-only`) are fixed in v1 and are likewise not configurable.

#### Separately owned code and board repositories

By default one repository owns both the Story's code and the board that tracks
it, and `worktree integrate` lands both in the control root. A shared product
board coordinating Stories whose code lives in other repositories has two
owners per delivery, and `board_repository` declares the second one:

```json
{
  "spawn": {
    "worktree_isolation": {
      "integration_base_branch": "main",
      "validation": { "commands": ["make test"] },
      "board_repository": {
        "root": "../product-board",
        "integration_base_branch": "main"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

- The control root is the **code owner**. Its accepted candidate lands through
  its own branch → pull request → review → checks → signed exact-head push,
  outside task-board. `worktree integrate` is refused with
  `board_owner_separate` while this block is present.
- `root` is the **board owner**: the main working tree of the repository the
  authoritative board directory (`TASK_BOARD_DIR`) resolves inside. A relative
  `root` resolves against the directory of the config file that was actually
  loaded — `TASK_BOARD_CONFIG` may place that file outside the control root —
  never against the control root or the working directory. `TASK_BOARD_DIR` is
  storage; the declaration plus these checks are the only Git authority, and
  an unreadable answer refuses (`board_owner_binding_invalid`).
- The two roots must be different working trees with different Git
  directories. A linked worktree of the code repository is refused; a second
  physical checkout of the **same canonical repository** is accepted, because
  the binding decides on physical roots, not on remote identity. Each checkout
  commits under its own configured human identity and signing key.
- `worktree complete <STORY-ID> --cr <ID> --revision <N> --landed-commit <SHA>`
  is the delivery. In order: it proves the declared commit is on the code
  repository's freshly observed protected default, carries the exact accepted
  candidate tree, verifies under the configured signer and is authored by the
  configured human identity; it projects the completed board bytes under the
  transaction's pinned write clock **without writing the authoritative board**;
  it builds the board-only commit from that projection on top of the board
  repository's fresh protected default and publishes it as a plain
  fast-forward; and only with that publication on record it applies the done
  transition through the same guarded transaction path `worktree integrate`
  uses. A refused push, a lost network or a crash anywhere up to the
  publication leaves the leaf and its Story `integrating`, and re-running the
  same command resumes. An accepted Change Request with `repository_delta`
  `empty` takes no `--landed-commit` and closes on the board commit alone,
  with no code validation invented for it.
- An accepted record with a repository delta but no validation evidence bound
  to its tree — one accepted under a configuration that declared no suite —
  is neither bypassed nor hand-edited: `worktree complete` revalidates the
  LANDED tree through the same materialize-and-validate route
  `worktree integrate` uses, records the evidence on the accepted revision,
  and refuses (`revalidation_failed`) when the configured suite fails on it.
- The board commit carries only the Story's own board paths and permitted
  ancestor aggregation. An ancestor aggregate the board repository's protected
  default advanced past what this checkout knows is **deferred**, reported as
  `shared_plane_deferred`, and never overwritten with a stale local rendering.
  Unrelated tracked, untracked and staged state in both checkouts is left
  where it is.
- The transaction reports itself as `code_landed_board_pending` until the
  publication is on record, `board_published_transition_pending` until the
  store's done transition is durable, then `board_published`. The board commit
  carries the owned transition progress AND activity bytes: the activity
  streams are previewed under a frozen mutation context and frozen event IDs,
  published, and then replayed byte-for-byte by the apply.

#### Run write boundary

`spawn.worktree_isolation.run_write_boundary` is `"warn"` or `"refuse"`.
Absent means `"warn"`. Any other value — including `null`, a number, or a
boolean — refuses config load with `invalid_spawn_config` naming
`spawn.worktree_isolation.run_write_boundary`.

The knob decides what happens when a tracked run writes outside its own
worktree (`.specs/run-write-boundary.md` owns the contract): under `refuse`
the run is marked `run_write_boundary_violated` and reviewer launch, verdict
acceptance (`accept_cr`, producer handoff) and delivery (`checkpoint`,
`integrate`, `complete`, `close-landed`) refuse until an operator clears that
run's marker with `task-board spawn write-boundary-clear <RUN-ID> --reason`;
under `warn` the same findings are reported loudly and the flow completes.
An unreadable config fails the gates closed: a malformed file, an unknown
knob value or an invalid sibling key refuses with `invalid_spawn_config`
(and records an indeterminate terminal slot) instead of warning — only a
genuinely absent file, block or knob takes the default.

The default is `warn` on purpose (design D12): concurrent orchestrator writes
share the run window, so attribution is conservative — a flagged path is not
always the run's own doing. Refusing runs on a conservative signal would
strand producers; `warn` reports loudly from day one while `refuse` is the
documented opt-in for campaigns that want the hard gate. Like every
`spawn.*` key, the knob may instead appear under `remote.spawn.*`, where it
rides the same effective-profile selection as the rest of the spawn policy.

#### The landing-gate suite

`task-board worktree integrate` gates on the validation suite
`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands`: an ordered command list that
must pass on the exact tree integration will land. The producer runs it at
Change Request build and records evidence keyed to the published candidate
tree; integration re-runs it when that binding is missing or broken. The digest
of the resolved list is the suite identity validation evidence binds to. See
[tracked background spawn](tracked-background-spawn.md#validation-binds-to-the-tree-that-lands).

The contract is three-valued rather than two-valued, and the middle value must
be written explicitly:

- An **absent** `validation` block (including `"validation": null`) refuses
  `worktree integrate` with `validation_not_configured` before any ref moves.
  The gate defaults to refusing, not to passing.
- An **explicitly empty** list — `"validation": {"commands": []}` — is a
  recorded decision to run nothing. The gate is satisfied without running a
  command, because an operator wrote that decision down.
- A **non-empty** list must pass, with every command exiting 0, on the tree
  that lands.

Each entry runs as its own foreground process and the gate stops at the first
non-zero exit. Large packages must therefore be split into exhaustive bounded
shards that finish inside the package and headless-run timeouts; backgrounding
a shard or redirecting away its real status is not evidence. The checked-in
project suite shards the board CLI's top-level `cmd` tests by test-name range
and runs its deliberately excluded Agy cases in a dedicated shard, so the split
reduces wall time without silently dropping coverage.

The shapes between those states are refused rather than coerced: a present
`validation` block without `commands`, or with `"commands": null`, is a config
error (`invalid_spawn_config`), because decoding either into the empty list
would turn an ambiguous shape into a landing pass nobody decided on. Blank
command strings and unknown keys inside the block are refused the same way.

The rest of the isolated workflow — provisioning, Change Requests, review,
rework, `worktree checkpoint` — does not read this key; only integration and
producer-side evidence recording do.

#### Board publication after the close

`spawn.worktree_isolation.board_publication` selects what `worktree
close-landed` and colocated `worktree integrate` do with the board state after
the done transition. Absent or null is `push`; any other value must be exactly
`push`, `commit_only` or `off`, and anything else refuses config load.

- `push` records the board-state commit on the local trunk and then pushes the
  board chain to the protected authority. The run ends in a published phase.
  A refused push returns `board_publication_pending` with the landing intact
  (done transition plus board-state commit durable) — run `task-board board
  publish` to finish the publication.
- `commit_only` records the board-state commit and pushes nothing. The run
  ends with the commit on the local trunk; `task-board board publish`
  publishes it later.
- `off` records no board-state commit and pushes nothing. On `close-landed`
  the close lands without touching trunk; on `integrate` the transaction's
  own board-state commit still lands, so `off` and `commit_only` differ only
  on `close-landed`.

A code landing reports `awaiting_hosted_merge` with exit 0 instead of
pending: a story commit cannot travel to the protected ref outside its
pull request, so a chain unproven only by this Story's own unmerged
story commits — of any generation, since a second generation lands on
top of an unmerged first — is the hosted-merge dependency, not a refused
push. The result keeps the story commit OID, the board commit OID and
the post-landing steps; once the hosted merge lands the story commits,
`task-board board publish` pushes the recorded board commit. A genuine
refusal of a code landing still owes that delivery: the pending envelope
carries the story commit OID and the post-landing steps beside the cause
and the remedy. `board_publication_pending` is reserved for a genuine
refusal of a provable chain — a rejected push, an overlap, a foreign
unproven link — with the landing intact. The `board publish` follow-up
holds for fast-forward and merge-commit hosted merges only: a squash or
rebase rewrite lands the story commit under a new identity, so the local
chain stays unproven at the original story commit and the follow-up
refuses with `board_publish_local_trunk_unproven`; re-anchoring a proven
board-record suffix over delivered story commits is follow-up work. A
pending prepared push from an earlier run still recovers before a
`close-landed` acknowledgement whatever the knob says: the knob governs
the close's own bytes only. With a declared separate board owner the
colocated wiring is out of scope and the close leaves publication to
`worktree complete`.

#### Trunk discovery, authority, and refusals

Protected-authority resolution is remote-only and remote-neutral — it never
assumes a remote named `origin` and never selects a ref from branch-owned or
cached evidence:

1. Resolve exactly one configured remote and canonicalize its URL. The remote
   name, canonical URL, binding source, and SHA-256 digest identify the
   repository.
2. Run a fresh `git ls-remote --symref <canonical-url> HEAD`. Accept exactly one
   well-formed `HEAD -> refs/heads/...` binding and exactly one HEAD OID.
3. Fetch that exact protected ref by canonical URL into a temporary private ref,
   resolve its commit, and require fetched and advertised OIDs to be equal.
4. Record the complete `ProtectedAuthorityTuple`: repository binding,
   protected ref, both OIDs, observation source/time, and raw-response digest.
   It is replay evidence only; every authority-sensitive production entry
   reacquires a new tuple.

Cached `refs/remotes/*/HEAD`, local `refs/heads/*`, branch tracking,
`integration_base_branch`, caller trunk fields, and earlier tuples never select
the protected ref or OID. Consequently, a dirty, ahead, or diverged control
checkout does not block provisioning and is not rewritten: the new Story is
seeded directly from the freshly fetched protected OID.

When `integration_base_branch` is configured, its branch name and complete
`branch.<name>.remote` / `branch.<name>.merge` pair are cross-checked against
the independently resolved protected branch/remote pair. The configured
branch's own tracking remote cannot redirect protected-authority discovery.

The legacy cache-only/advisory discovery helpers retain their no-remote current
branch behavior. They are not an authorization path. Every failure mode in live
protected-authority resolution is a typed, fail-closed refusal:

| Condition | Refusal | Remedy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Zero configured remotes or the authorized URL/HEAD cannot be read or fetched | `worktree_protected_authority_unavailable` | Restore the unique repository binding and remote access; a read failure is not absence and never enables local fallback. |
| Multiple configured remotes, malformed/multiple/missing symref records, missing HEAD OID, unreadable fetched OID, or advertised/fetched disagreement | `worktree_protected_authority_indeterminate` | Remove ambiguity or repair remote metadata/objects; no candidate is authorized while the observation is inconsistent. |
| Configured branch/remote/merge pair differs from the independently resolved protected default | `worktree_configured_trunk_not_protected_default` | Align `integration_base_branch` and its tracking pair with repository policy, or remove the stale pin. |

`ResolveTrunk` remains as a compatibility/advisory API, not as a live
protected-authority selector. Its typed outcomes are
`worktree_trunk_ambiguous`, `worktree_integration_base_missing`,
`worktree_trunk_authority_unresolved`,
`worktree_trunk_authority_refresh_failed`,
`worktree_trunk_local_ahead_of_authority`, and
`worktree_trunk_diverged_from_authority`. Separately,
`worktree_ref_unreadable` remains live in managed-workspace validation when a
ref lookup or ancestry check cannot be established. None of these outcomes
enables a local, cached, configured, or caller-provided fallback for a decision
that requires fresh protected authority.

These refusals are evaluated by `ResolveProtectedAuthority` and the configured
cross-check wrapper. Provision, Change Request acceptance, Integrate entry and
fresh-state validation, pre-materialization revalidation, and final-leaf
refresh invoke that resolver at their own authority-sensitive boundaries.
There is no memoization API: a stored tuple cannot be labeled fresh. The spawn
plan and advisory Story-activation reminder may still use read-only/cache-only
projections (`DiscoverTrunk` / `DiscoverCachedTrunk`) for display, but those
answers never authorize provisioning, acceptance, materialization, or landing.

**Local boards only.** The managed workspace path requires a local authoritative
board. On a remote board, provisioning and every `task-board worktree` command
refuse with `worktree_isolation_requires_local_board` before any side effect,
and remote mode otherwise keeps its existing behaviour. No configuration
disables that refusal. The reason is data locality: board-path admission
compares control-root worktree bytes against the authoritative store's current
rendering, and in remote mode no single process holds both operands — the client
has no store and board-server has no checkout. The block itself still parses
under `remote.spawn.*`; the refusal is a runtime capability precondition, not a
parse error, so the operator gets a named, actionable message.

### `spawn-policy-v2`

`spawn.ceilings` is keyed by runtime ID: any syntactically valid identifier
(lowercase letters/digits/hyphen/underscore, starting with a letter) parses
structurally, including one this document predates. `codex`, `claude`, `qwen`,
`muse`, `agy`, and `gemini` are the shipped (built-in) runtimes and each is
optional. A key naming any OTHER runtime ID must first be declared under
[`spawn.runtimes`](#declaring-runtimes), or the ceiling is refused as
`spawn_runtime_undeclared` naming the exact `spawn.ceilings.<id>` key — a
syntactically valid but undeclared identifier is not admission, only a
spellable one.

| Runtime/role key | Type | Required | Allowed/default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `model` | non-empty model ID string | Runtime-specific | No default | Compatibility-snapshot model bound. |
| `model_criterion` | string | When required by the configured bound | `equal`, `less_or_equal`, `greater_or_equal` | Comparison direction. |
| `reasoning_effort` | string | Runtime-specific | Codex: `minimal`, `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `max`, `ultra`; Qwen: `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `max`; Claude: `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `max` (own vocabulary; no `ultra`, and legacy Opus/Sonnet 4.6 rows also lack `xhigh`) | Effort bound. |
| `fast_mode` | boolean | No; Codex provider only | `false` | Resolved false forces `service_tier="default"`; true forces `service_tier="priority"`. Role blocks and every non-Codex runtime reject the key. |
| `roles.<role>.model` | model ID string | No | Inherits runtime | Role override. |
| `roles.<role>.model_criterion` | criterion string | No | Inherits runtime | Role override. |
| `roles.<role>.reasoning_effort` | effort string | No | Inherits runtime | Role override. |
| `roles.<role>.default_model` | model ID string | No | No default | Role selection default; see [Per-role model preferences](#per-role-model-preferences). |
| `roles.<role>.default_reasoning_effort` | effort string | No | No default | Role selection default; Codex/Qwen/Claude only. |

Role names must be supported task-board roles; `coordinator` canonicalizes to
`orchestrator`.

### `spawn-policy-v3` / `spawn-policy-v4`

Each configured runtime declares an explicit admission set; model registry
rank/order does not grant admission.

`spawn-policy-v3` and `spawn-policy-v4` are two `contract_version` spellings
of the exact same wire shape: every key, input form, and validation rule
documented below applies identically to both. v4 adds no new field and
changes no admission behavior — it exists only as the version new
[v2 migration](#migrating-from-spawn-policy-v2) guidance now targets (decision
D8). A repository already on `spawn-policy-v3` is unaffected and undeprecated
this release; there is no requirement, automatic or otherwise, to move an
existing v3 config to v4.

The admission authority is the **admitted `(model, effort)` pair set**, not any
single config key. A runtime declares it with exactly one of two mutually
exclusive input forms:

- **`allowed_models`** — a flat model list under one shared effort bound. This is
  the degenerate case, adequate whenever every admitted model may run at the
  same efforts.
- **`entries`** — a list of combinable entries whose union is the pair set. Use
  it when different models need different effort bounds, which a flat list plus
  one shared bound provably cannot express: `equal(gpt-5.6-sol, low)` UNION
  `equal(gpt-5.3-codex-spark, xhigh)` has no `allowed_models` equivalent, since
  a shared bound of `xhigh` over-admits Sol and one of `low` under-admits Spark.

Both forms translate into the same pair set, and `project_config()` reports it
as `admitted_pairs`. Read that field rather than `allowed_models` when you need
to know what a policy actually admits: `allowed_models` is `null` under the
entries form, and even under its own form it cannot say which efforts survived
the bound.

| Runtime/role key | Type | Required | Allowed/default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `allowed_models` | non-empty array of unique model IDs | One admission form is required per runtime; optional role replacement | None | Flat input form; mutually exclusive with `entries`. |
| `entries` | non-empty array of entry objects | One admission form is required per runtime; optional role replacement | None | Combinable input form; the union of the entries is the admitted pair set. |
| `entries_expansion_digest` | `sha256:...` string | Required when an entry uses `less_or_equal`/`greater_or_equal` | None | Pins the ordered expansion; written only by `task-board spawn ceilings pin`. |
| `entries_expansion_pairs` | array of `model` or `model:effort` strings | Required beside the digest | None | Lets drift be reported as exact added/removed pairs. |
| `resolution_model` | admitted model ID string or `null` in a role | No | No fallback | Exact fallback when adjustment is allowed; must appear in the pair set. |
| `adjustment_confirmation` | string | Yes for runtime; optional role override | `required`, `none` | Whether adjusted resolution needs acknowledgement. |
| `reasoning_effort` | string | No | Runtime default | Shared effort bound; not accepted beside `entries`. |
| `reasoning_effort_criterion` | string | Required with a directional effort bound | `less_or_equal`, `greater_or_equal` | Effort direction; not accepted beside `entries`. |
| `fast_mode` | boolean | No; Codex provider only | `false` | Launch tier policy. Omitted/false map to `default`; true maps to `priority`; ambient Codex tier settings cannot override it. |
| `roles.<role>.*` | provider fields except `fast_mode` | No | Inherit runtime field-by-field | Role replacement/override. `fast_mode` remains provider-scoped and is rejected here. |
| `roles.<role>.default_model` | model ID string | No | No default | Role selection default; see [Per-role model preferences](#per-role-model-preferences). |
| `roles.<role>.default_reasoning_effort` | effort string | No | No default | Role selection default; Codex/Qwen/Claude only. |

`fast_mode` is resolved from the same authoritative whole `spawn` object as the
ceiling: root `spawn.ceilings.codex.fast_mode` in local/root-fallback mode and
`remote.spawn.ceilings.codex.fast_mode` when a non-null remote spawn profile is
active. Spawn-preflight reports the effective boolean, `default` versus
`configured` source, and exact provenance key. The selection audit, run state,
and manifest persist the same resolved value. At the Codex CLI boundary only,
`true` becomes `service_tier="priority"` and `false` becomes
`service_tier="default"`; neither direct nor managed launches inherit a parent
session, environment, or user-config tier.

#### Ceiling entries

Each entry is `{criterion, model, reasoning_effort?}`. The union is a plain OR:
entry order is not significant, and **no entry can subtract from another**.

For a single Astra lane capped at the lowest supported effort, use an explicit
v4 allow-set. This admits `gpt-6-astra/low`; requesting a higher effort requires
the normal selection rationale and exact resolved-pair acknowledgement:

See the complete [Astra ceiling snapshot](assets/task-board-config/local-spawn-policy-v4-astra.json).

| Entry key | Type | Required | Allowed | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `criterion` | string | Yes | `equal`, `less_or_equal`, `greater_or_equal` | Governs **both** dimensions of the entry. |
| `model` | runtime-owned model ID | Yes | — | The bound both dimensions are read against. |
| `reasoning_effort` | string | No | Runtime effort vocabulary | Effort bound; omitted bounds no effort. Rejected for Gemini and Agy (Antigravity encodes effort in the model ID). Accepted for Muse from `high`, `xhigh`, `max` — the vocabulary `muse-spark-1.3-contributor` and its `muse-spark` alias carry, and REQUIRE ([Muse readiness](../.specs/spawn-model-selection.md#muse-readiness)). A bare Codex bound still admits `minimal` for the legacy `gpt-5.x-codex` rows, but `gpt-6-astra`/`astra` and `gpt-6-sol`/`sol` carry the six-word `low`/`medium`/`high`/`xhigh`/`max`/`ultra` axis and `gpt-6-luna`/`luna` the five-word `low`..`max` one, and all refuse it ([Astra readiness](../.specs/spawn-model-selection.md#astra-readiness)). |

The criterion governs the effort dimension too, so an entry reads as
`criterion(model, reasoning_effort)`:

- `equal(gpt-5.3-codex-spark, xhigh)` admits Spark **at `xhigh` and nowhere
  else**. A Spark request below it is refused, not lifted.
- `less_or_equal(gpt-5.6-luna, high)` admits every model at or below Luna by
  capability rank, at every effort at or below `high`.
- `greater_or_equal(gpt-5.6-terra, high)` is the mirror: at or above, in both
  dimensions.

One criterion for both dimensions is what makes the pair set exact. Under a
hardcoded effort ceiling no input form could ever admit a model at a single
effort, and `equal(sol, low)` UNION `equal(spark, xhigh)` would silently admit
`spark/low`.

A role that configures `entries` replaces the runtime's admission form
wholesale — the inherited `allowed_models` **and** the inherited runtime effort
bound are dropped, because each entry already carries its own bound. Omitting
both forms in a role inherits the runtime policy unchanged.

```json
{
  "spawn": {
    "ceilings": {
      "contract_version": "spawn-policy-v3",
      "codex": {
        "entries": [
          {"criterion": "equal", "model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low"},
          {"criterion": "equal", "model": "gpt-5.3-codex-spark", "reasoning_effort": "xhigh"}
        ],
        "resolution_model": "gpt-5.6-sol",
        "adjustment_confirmation": "none"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

#### Pinning an ordered expansion

`less_or_equal` and `greater_or_equal` resolve model membership by comparing
runtime-local capability rank. Capability rank is evidence, not an admission
authority, so an ordered expansion may only be used once an operator has
approved the exact pair set it produces:

```bash
task-board spawn ceilings pin              # every configured runtime and role
task-board spawn ceilings pin --agent codex --role reviewer
```

This command is the only writer of `entries_expansion_digest`; nothing writes it
implicitly. Until it runs, an ordered entry set fails closed with
`spawn_ceiling_admission_drift` naming the config key and the next step. If a
later, truthful re-rank moves the expansion, the same failure lists the exact
added and removed pairs — admission never widens or narrows silently. `--agent`
or `--role` matching no configured entry target is an error naming the selector,
not a successful no-op.

`equal`-only entry sets never consult capability rank, cannot drift, and are
never pinned; configuring a digest for one is rejected.

### Migrating from `spawn-policy-v2`

Nothing rewrites `task-board.config.json` automatically — a `spawn-policy-v2`
repository stays byte-identical until an operator edits it by hand. `task-board
q 'project_config()'` still reports the migration evidence needed to do that
edit, under `spawn.ceilings`:

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `migration_required` | `true` whenever any v2 runtime is configured. |
| `migration_target` | `"spawn-policy-v4"` — the version a v2 repository is now steered to, per decision D8. `spawn-policy-v3` remains a fully supported target too; it is simply no longer the one this field names. |
| `<runtime>.migration` | The exact `spawn-policy-v4`-shaped runtime object — `allowed_models`, `resolution_model`, `adjustment_confirmation`, configured `reasoning_effort`/`reasoning_effort_criterion`, and provider-level Codex `fast_mode` when explicitly configured — that preserves the effective v2 ceiling. Provider migration keeps `fast_mode`; role migration objects omit it because role blocks reject the field. Paste the object under the corresponding provider (or role) once `contract_version` is set to `spawn-policy-v4`; no field needs editing first. |
| `<runtime>.migration_warning.reason` | `spawn_policy_v2_explicit_migration_required` (a `model`/`model_criterion` bound is configured) or `spawn_policy_v2_effort_only_migration_required` (only `reasoning_effort` is). Frozen: a caller may key on either string across releases. |
| `<runtime>.migration_warning.next_step` | Human-readable instructions. Not frozen — only the `reason` codes above are a stable contract. |

The projected object is computed through the same frozen
`spawn-policy-v2-rollout-*` compatibility snapshot the live v2 resolver
enforces (never live capability rank), so pasting it produces the identical
admitted `(model, effort)` pair set the v2 ceiling already granted — verified
by expanding both through the real admission pipeline, not by inspection.

### Declaring runtimes

`spawn.runtimes` declares an additional `(agentic system, broker)` binding
beyond the six frozen built-ins (`claude`, `codex`, `qwen`, `gemini`, `agy`,
`muse`). Omitting the block entirely — the state of every config written
before this section existed — leaves every other block semantically
identical: `spawn.ceilings`, `spawn.limits.backoff` and
`preferred_agentic_system` already accept any syntactically valid built-in
runtime ID with no declaration, and this block only adds vocabulary a ceiling
may then reference, it never narrows what a built-in runtime could already
do.

```json
{
  "spawn": {
    "runtimes": {
      "local-qwen": {
        "agentic_system": "pi",
        "broker": "local-models",
        "publisher": "alibaba",
        "family": "qwen",
        "backend": "mlx",
        "provider": "local-qwen",
        "model": "qwen-3.8-27b-mlx-8bit",
        "profile": "qwen-3.8-27b-mlx-8bit"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

| Key | Type | Required | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `agentic_system` | string | Yes | The harness that runs this runtime's agent loop. Must be one this release ships a launch adapter for: `claude-code`, `codex`, `qwen-code`, `gemini-cli`, `antigravity`, `muse`, or `pi`. |
| `broker` | string | Yes | The backend that serves the model and owns the account, e.g. `anthropic`, `openai`, `alibaba`, or a new one such as `moonshot`. Brokers are open — naming a new one needs no code change. |
| `home_env` | string | No | The environment variable holding this runtime's account home. Omitted, the harness adapter's default home rule applies, aliasing the account with the built-in runtime sharing the same `agentic_system` — legal, and correct when the operator genuinely shares one login. |
| `publisher` / `family` | string | No | Public model provenance. These fields do not select a broker; for local Qwen they are `alibaba` / `qwen`. |
| `backend` | string | No | Replaceable inference implementation metadata, for example `mlx`; it is not a vendor or broker. |
| `provider` | string | No | Profile-provider identity recorded in launch/run provenance, for example `local-qwen`. |
| `model` / `profile` | string | No | Concrete, versioned deployment selection. Stable runtime IDs must not contain the model version. |

`local-qwen` is the managed local-model example. The client resolves
`pi -> local-models` through the agents-management registries, performs broker
availability/preflight locally, and persists the public identity snapshot in
the tracked run manifest. In remote board mode the spawning client still owns
that work: the board server stores board/run data but never probes or claims to
host the local model. Private model paths, sockets, process identifiers and
broker credentials are not config or query fields.

**Declaring a built-in runtime.** A `spawn.runtimes` key naming one of the six
built-ins is legal exactly when its `agentic_system`/`broker` match the
built-in binding exactly — this is idempotent, not merely tolerated. A
DIFFERENT binding for a built-in ID is refused as
`spawn_runtime_contradicts_builtin`, naming the dotted key and both bindings:
redeclaring `codex` with `broker: "alibaba"` would otherwise silently
redirect codex's admitted-pair digests, limit-state identity and quota
accounting onto an account it never used.

**Why a matching declaration is legal (the F2 decision).** A later release
that promotes an operator-declared runtime ID to built-in keeps every config
that already declared that exact binding working untouched. The alternative,
a reserved namespace that refuses any declared ID a future release might
claim, breaks every operator who followed the documented `spawn.runtimes`
path at the exact moment they upgrade. Nothing about the built-in table
changes when a declaration matches it; the declaration becomes redundant, not
invalid.

**What a declaration is not: the honest qwen-codex accounting.** `qwen-codex`
(the alibaba broker driven by the codex harness) is this epic's own worked
example, registered as its acceptance test. Config plus a registry row was
**not** sufficient by itself. Declaring `spawn.runtimes.qwen-codex` made the
runtime *selectable* (it parses, it enumerates under `--agent`, its ceiling
key is accepted) but launching a real model through it needed four small,
additive code changes this section's own contract predicts nothing about:

- the model registry needed a way for two runtimes to share one
  `agentic_system` (`codex`) without colliding — every `ModelInfo` row now
  declares its own `Runtime` directly rather than deriving it by reverse
  lookup, which is exactly what two runtimes sharing a harness broke;
- the model index needed a real duplicate-ID guard, because reusing an
  existing model ID under a second runtime would have silently moved the
  *first* runtime's registration out from under it;
- the spawn plane's `ProviderLimitID` needed its own case for the new
  runtime, or a qwen-codex quota observation would have aliased with plain
  codex's and silently cleared or suppressed the real account's state;
- the account-home rule table needed a third entry, or `qwen-codex` simply
  had no home to resolve.

None of these are hacks: they are gaps the config contract alone cannot
close, because `spawn.runtimes` only ever promised vocabulary, not behavior.
Two things were deliberately left unresolved rather than guessed:

- the pre-existing `qwen` (qwen-code) runtime still has no account-home rule;
  no evidence in this tree establishes which environment variable the real
  qwen-code CLI reads for its config home, and inventing one would be exactly
  the kind of guessed evidence this contract refuses to ship;
- `home_env` is validated at config-parse time but consumed nowhere else —
  it is not injected into the child process environment and not consulted by
  the limit plane's identity resolution
  (`skill-agents-management/pkg/providerlimits`). An operator who wants a runtime
  declared under `spawn.runtimes` to run under a genuinely separate,
  simultaneous account from the built-in runtime sharing its
  `agentic_system` cannot get that today. What works is the default-alias
  case only: point the shared account-home env var (for example `CODEX_HOME`
  for any `codex`-harnessed runtime) at the account to use, one account at a
  time, before spawning under the declared runtime — the same convention
  already used to switch between two plain `codex` logins.

**Refusals.**

| Reason code | Meaning | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `spawn_runtime_contradicts_builtin` | A `spawn.runtimes` entry redeclares a built-in runtime ID with a different `agentic_system` or `broker`. | Remove the entry, or make it match the built-in binding exactly. |
| `spawn_runtime_unknown_agentic_system` | A declared `agentic_system` has no shipped launch adapter this release. | Use one of the agentic systems named in the refusal, or wait for a release that ships the adapter. |
| `spawn_runtime_undeclared` | A ceiling (or another runtime-referencing block) names a runtime ID that is neither built in nor declared under `spawn.runtimes`. | Add a `spawn.runtimes.<id>` entry, or correct the identifier. |
| `invalid_spawn_runtime_config` | A `spawn.runtimes` entry is malformed: not an object, or missing/empty `agentic_system`/`broker`. | Supply both required fields as non-empty strings. |

### Rate-limit knobs

`spawn.limits.backoff` is the per-broker suppression ladder: how long a
broker's quota stays suppressed after a spawn observes it is exhausted, before
the next probe. Every field is optional, and an absent `spawn.limits` block
applies every default silently.

```json
{
  "spawn": {
    "limits": {
      "backoff": {
        "anthropic": ["2m", "5m", "15m", "30m", "60m", "120m"],
        "alibaba":   ["5m", "15m", "60m"]
      },
      "groups": {
        "alibaba-token-plan": ["qwen3.7-plus", "qwen3.7-max"]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

| Key | Type | Required | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `backoff.<key>` | array of duration strings | No | `["2m","5m","15m","30m","60m","120m"]` per broker | Suppression ladder; steps must be strictly positive, `<= 6h`, and non-decreasing. |
| `probe_lease_minutes` | integer `[1, 120]` | No | `10` | Probe-lease reconfirmation interval. |
| `probe_claim_max_minutes` | integer `[1, 240]` | No | `30` | Whole pre-launch claim cap; must be `>=` the lease. |
| `spread` | `round_robin`, `preference`, or `affinity` | No | `round_robin` | Distribution mode over surviving runtimes. |
| `groups.<group>` | array of model IDs | No | Static table | Replaces (never merges into) one subscription-group row. |

**The `backoff` key is a broker, not a runtime.** A throttle ladder is an
account property: two runtimes sharing one broker (for example a future
second Anthropic-backed runtime) share one ladder. A key is recognized when
it is either:

- the broker of a built-in runtime (`anthropic`, `openai`, `alibaba`, or
  `google` for `gemini`/`agy`), or
- the broker of a runtime declared under [`spawn.runtimes`](#declaring-runtimes)
  in the same config (this is what makes the `moonshot` row in the
  `kimi-codex` example above meaningful).

A key that resolves neither way — `"gemini"` alone, with no declaration
naming it, is the concrete example — is reported as an advisory
`config_section_unknown` diagnostic and its ladder is dropped, exactly as an
unrecognized `spawn.limits.backoff` key has always been handled; it is never a
refusal by itself.

**The one-release legacy spelling.** `codex`, `claude`, and `qwen` — the three
runtime IDs a backoff row could already legally be keyed by before this
section existed — keep working for one release, resolved through the frozen
built-in table (`codex`→`openai`, `claude`→`anthropic`, `qwen`→`alibaba`).
Using one emits a `config_section_legacy` diagnostic naming the exact key.
Unlike the unrecognized-key case above this is not advisory (the value is not
being dropped) and it does not block the load either:
`spawn.limits.backoff.codex` continues to configure codex's ladder exactly as
it always did, and the diagnostic exists only so an operator moving to the
broker spelling can find every row that still needs it.

**Both spellings for the same broker is a refusal, not a merge.**
`{"backoff": {"codex": [...], "openai": [...]}}` is refused as
`spawn_backoff_key_conflict`, naming both dotted keys and the broker they
share. Keep exactly one row per broker — prefer the broker spelling.

### Two-tier launch deadline

`spawn.deadline.hard_multiplier` is a number `>= 1` (default `3`). For a
spawn carrying `--timeout`, that flag is the planned budget; the terminating
hard fence is planned time multiplied by this value. `--hard-timeout` or
`--deadline-multiplier` overrides the configured derivation for one spawn.
Setting `hard_multiplier: 1` preserves the legacy single-deadline behavior.

At the planned budget the run records `soft_deadline_crossed` and sends one
in-session check-in through the run's production delivery channel. The child
records `on_track` or `failing` with `task-board spawn checkin RUN --assessment
... --detail ...`. A missing answer after one additional planned-budget window
records `soft_deadline_unassessed`; it never terminates the run. `spawn status`
and compact `spawn observe` expose planned, hard, elapsed, crossing, and latest
assessment fields. If the host refuses the check-in, the run records
`soft_deadline_delivery_failed`, remains live, and retries once at the grace
tick. Delivery attempts, successful delivery time, and the last delivery error
remain visible in status/observe rather than being reported as a delivered
crossing.

This split is grounded in the 2026-09-15/16 witnesses: Codex reworks were killed
at 90m and 60m under load even though work was moving, followed by status-bar
and `d9jhm4` continuation respawns.

### Supervision (minutes)

Supervision is a ceiling dimension like the model bounds, resolved per runtime
and per role from the same object, and configured in **minutes**. It answers a
different question from the model bounds: not *what may this run*, but *how long
does the orchestrator stay obliged to it*.

```jsonc
"codex": {
  "entries": [ /* … */ ],
  "supervision": {
    "check_every_minutes": 5,        // soft: how often the supervisor samples. Free.
    "idle_deadline_minutes": 90,     // hard: no child-authored board progress for this long
    "max_runtime_minutes": 0,        // optional absolute cap, 0 = off
    "on_deadline": "kill_and_park"   // park | kill_and_park | cancel
  },
  "roles": { "reviewer": { "supervision": { "idle_deadline_minutes": 45 } } }
}
```

| Key | Type | Required | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `check_every_minutes` | integer minutes | No | `5` | Sample period. Must be `>= 1` and must not exceed **either** armed bound — `idle_deadline_minutes` or `max_runtime_minutes` — because a sampler coarser than a bound cannot enforce it. The comparison is made on the **effective** policy, never on a partial block: the runtime block with its omitted fields defaulted (it is what every role that overrides nothing runs under), and each configured role as its field-wise merge. |
| `idle_deadline_minutes` | integer minutes | No | `0` (off) | Minutes without child-authored board progress before `on_deadline` fires. |
| `max_runtime_minutes` | integer minutes | No | `0` (off) | Absolute wall clock before `on_deadline` fires regardless of progress. |
| `on_deadline` | `park`, `kill_and_park`, `cancel` | No | `park` | What happens when a bound is crossed. |

Role blocks merge **field-wise** over the runtime block, by the same
omission-inherits rule the v3 allow-set role override uses. A role that shortens
only `idle_deadline_minutes` keeps the runtime's interval and mode; replacing
the block wholesale would quietly downgrade a configured `kill_and_park` back to
`park`. An explicit `0` is a configured value ("off"), distinct from omission.

Validation follows the same rule, in two steps. Each configured block is judged
on its **shape** alone — ranges, a non-zero sample interval, a known
`on_deadline` — with no defaults manufactured for the fields it omitted.
Enforceability is then judged only on **effective** policies. Substituting the
package default for a field a role omitted would refuse
`runtime check_every_minutes: 1` plus `reviewer idle_deadline_minutes: 2`,
which the sampler enforces exactly as written; the same role block under
`runtime check_every_minutes: 3` is still refused.

**Defaults when unconfigured are `check_every_minutes: 5` and
`idle_deadline_minutes: 0`.** Shipping a default-on kill would discard in-flight
paid work in every repository on upgrade. The bound that is *not* optional lives
in the recovery-successor chain, not here.

`task-board q 'project_config()'` reports the effective supervision block for
every runtime and configured role under `spawn.ceilings.<runtime>.supervision`,
with a `provenance` map naming the config key each field came from. A field with
no provenance entry came from the defaults above.

#### The deadline is idle-based, and what counts as progress

`idle_deadline_minutes` measures **idleness, not total runtime**. Reading state
is nearly free; killing at minute 30 discards 30 minutes of paid tokens. A
total-runtime cap kills good long runs, an idle cap kills stalled ones — so the
absolute cap is the separate, default-off `max_runtime_minutes`.

`last_progress_at` is computed from **run events only, from this run's own log**.
Element timestamps are never read, which is what makes authorship decidable: a
run event exists only because something acted on *this* run.

| Counts as progress | Does not count |
| --- | --- |
| `progress` minted by the stamping rule † | `heartbeat` — the supervisor's own sample |
| `directive_acknowledged` †  | `queued`, `slot_claimed` — scheduler bookkeeping |
| `goal_created`, `goal_revised` † | `directive_requested` — **operator**-authored |
| `reviewer_verdict_recorded` † | `progress` written by the **runner** narrating itself |
| `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled`, `late_completion` | any event class this build does not recognise |

If heartbeats counted, the supervisor would be refreshing the deadline it is
enforcing, and a wedged process that keeps emitting them would be immortal.

**† Class is not authorship.** The starred classes are also reachable from
someone other than the child, so being in the eligible set is necessary and not
sufficient: they count only when the event records that the **owning child**
authored it.

`progress` is the sharpest case, because its loudest producer is the runner
itself. The runner writes `progress` events to narrate its own scheduling —
"Runner slot claimed", "Spawned agent is executing", "Retrying deferred
successor start" — from the very process that enforces the deadline. A rule
that admitted `progress` on class alone therefore let a wedged child be kept
alive by its own supervisor's commentary, and excluding only *repeated*
observations did not help, because that narration never repeats. `progress`
counts only when it carries the stamping rule's `child_mutation` origin, which
was minted inside the board mutation lock after proving that a process carrying
this run's id changed persisted content. Runner narration stays fully visible in
`spawn events`, marked `runner_observation`; it simply is not the child acting.
`task-board spawn directive RUN-... request_progress` on a running run marks the
operator's own directive acknowledged the instant it is stored — the
acknowledgement means "recorded for the child to poll", not "the child
answered" — and `task-board spawn goal upsert RUN-...` run without
`TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` activates a revision as an operator. Authorship is decided
the same structural way the stamping rule decides it: the acting process
carried this run's `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID`. A sibling run's child, a system actor,
and an event carrying no recorded author are all foreign — an absent author is
not read as the child. Without this, the act of checking on a stalled run would
keep it alive.

**The stamping rule.** After a board mutation commits, the CLI emits exactly one
`progress` run event **iff** both hold:

1. `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` is set in the mutating process and names a **live** run.
   Every spawned child carries it; an operator's own shell does not, and a
   different child carries a different one. Authorship is structural, not
   inferred.
2. The mutation **changed persisted content**. A no-op — `set_status` to the
   current status, `assign` to the current agent — writes no event. Otherwise
   "progress" would be one idempotent call away from meaningless.

The change is decided by a board content fingerprint compared **inside the board
mutation lock**, around the write itself. The `## Last Update` stamp every
progress write refreshes is normalized out, and recovery bookkeeping whose value
moves on every write is skipped. Taking the comparison inside the lock is what
makes it authorship evidence rather than timing: no other process can commit
while it is open, so a concurrent operator write or a sibling run's mutation is
never credited to the observed child.

The event is stamped on the run named by `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` **whatever element
was written**, so a child creating follow-up tasks on a sibling Story still
counts.

The rule is that **every command that persists board content locally stamps**,
not a judgement call about which commands a child is likely to run. The
enumerated surface is `m`, `handoff`, `resource add`, `resource update`,
`resource delete`, `repair-links`, `import`, and `primary-goal set/update/clear`.
`resource delete` in particular is a separate command from the DSL's
`delete_resource`, and coverage of one is not coverage of the other: a child
retiring a superseded artifact changed the board, and a supervisor that cannot
see it parks a run that is working. Read-only commands (`q`, `grep`, `tree`,
`plan`, `validate`, `export`) and commands that write outside the board
(`setup-config`, `login`, `worktree`) do not stamp.

A stamped event is **never** discarded as a repeated observation, however
identical its rendering. `resource update` renders one fixed summary, so ten
genuine revisions of one artifact produce ten identical-looking events — and
they are ten changes. The "unchanged `status`/`phase` observation" exclusion
above applies only to the runner's own phase samples. The two mistakes are not
symmetric: a false park destroys paid work, a missed park only delays a kill.

#### `on_deadline`

| Mode | Child | Run | Scheduler slot |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `park` *(default)* | left alive | `failed` + `completion_reason=supervision_parked` + `orphaned_child_pid` | released |
| `kill_and_park` | SIGTERM, 30 s grace, SIGKILL | `failed` + `completion_reason=supervision_parked` | released |
| `cancel` | killed as above | `cancelled` + `completion_reason=supervision_cancelled` | released |

`park` is the default because parking costs nothing and loses nothing: it bounds
the *supervisor's obligation*, not the child. **The honest tradeoff: a parked run
releases its scheduler slot while its process may still be alive, so the real
process count can exceed `max_parallel`.** `orphaned_child_pid` is recorded
precisely so an operator can act on that; `task-board spawn status` prints it.
`kill_and_park` is the recommended setting for unattended waves.

All three modes route the board element — a note plus the actionable recovery
status — so a supervised run is never silently parked.

**What a terminating mode attests, and what it does not.** Both signals address
the child's process *group*, so the confirmation asks the same question: after
the hard kill, does that group still have a live member? Only a group with none
left is reported as terminated, and the record says which identity was
confirmed — `child terminated (process group 4821 confirmed gone)`. Confirming
against the leader pid instead would answer a question nobody asked: a runtime
wrapper exits the instant it forks, so the leader is gone within milliseconds
while the worker doing the work is still a member of the group. A supervisor
that read leader liveness therefore skipped SIGKILL entirely and recorded a
termination over a live paid process. When the group cannot be proven empty, the
run says so — `could not be confirmed terminated (process group 4821 still had a
live member after the termination attempt) and may still be running` — and
records `orphaned_child_pid`, which is the pgid an operator can signal by hand.
Unknown is reported as unknown; it is never rounded down to death.

#### The launch fence

The table above describes what happens to a child that already exists. The
supervisor is deliberately started *before* the launch — a bound that only arms
once a child exists cannot bound a launch that hangs — so a deadline can also
fire while the launch is still composing and there is no handle to stop.

Every verdict raises a fence before it signals anything:

- **No new runtime work starts.** Each launch shape checks the fence where
  money starts being spent — process start for the ordinary prompt-mode
  launcher, session attach for a managed owner — and reports a non-recoverable
  `launch fenced` result instead of starting. Non-recoverable on purpose: a
  recoverable signal there is how one deadline becomes a successor chain
  retrying the same wall clock.
- **A handle published anyway is dealt with immediately**, not at the next
  sample. `kill_and_park` and `cancel` terminate it; `park` leaves it alive, on
  the same reasoning as the table, and records it as `orphaned_child_pid` /
  `orphaned_child_session` together with the reason, so a late child is never
  known only to a warning line.
- **A child that is already running is a separate decision from the fence.** The
  fence answers one question — may new runtime work start? — and every mode
  answers it the same way, *no*. What happens to work already running is the
  mode's decision, and the launcher asks for it separately: `park` stops waiting
  without signalling anything, so the run goes terminal and releases its slot
  while the child keeps working; `kill_and_park` and `cancel` reach the launch
  path's termination handler as before. One signal carrying both questions is
  what once made `park` SIGTERM the child it had just promised to leave alone.
  The same split applies to a managed owner: `park` stops waiting on the
  assignment instead of cancelling its run context.

#### Named limitations

These are stated rather than papered over.

- **A worker that leaves the process group cannot be proven dead.** Termination
  addresses the child's process *group*, which reaches every worker a runtime
  wrapper forks normally. A wrapper that starts its worker with `setsid` puts it
  outside that group while it still holds the launcher's inherited output
  descriptor. The launcher bounds its own wait rather than blocking on a process
  its signals cannot reach, and then reports `escaped_child` — *"could not be
  proven terminated and may still be running"*. It does **not** claim a kill it
  did not witness. The deadline bounds the launcher, which is what the deadline
  is for; the surviving worker is surfaced for an operator exactly like an
  orphaned pid. Supervision's own claim is scoped to match: it reports the
  *group* it signalled as gone, never the whole tree, because a process that
  left the group is outside what any group-addressed probe can see.

- **A parked child is left alone, not made immortal.** `park` guarantees that
  this product does not signal the child. It does not guarantee the child lives
  forever: the runner process owns the pipe the child writes through, so once
  the runner exits, a parked child that keeps writing can still die of `SIGPIPE`
  from the operating system. The launcher therefore leaves the log handle open
  on the way out, so whatever the orphan writes in the meantime still lands in
  the run log an operator reads before acting on `orphaned_child_pid`.

- **The idle deadline is not a liveness oracle.** A child that writes a genuine
  board change every few minutes while making no real headway keeps its deadline
  alive indefinitely. Mid-run content is not observable on any board surface —
  `claude -p --output-format json` emits nothing until its final envelope, and
  `spawn events` reports only status and phase — so supervision cannot check
  *direction*, only whether the child is acting at all. The two available
  answers are both in this design and both optional: `max_runtime_minutes`, and
  the product-enforced recovery-successor chain bound.
- **Remote boards cannot prove child-authored progress, so an idle deadline is
  refused there.** The stamping rule compares board content inside the *local*
  mutation lock; a remote board commits on the server, where the client has
  nothing to compare, so a remote child produces no eligible progress event
  however hard it works. `task-board spawn` **refuses to launch** when
  `idle_deadline_minutes` is armed in remote mode, rather than warning and
  arming a deadline that would measure elapsed time and park working children.
  Use `max_runtime_minutes` on a remote board — elapsed time needs no authorship
  proof — or run the wave against a local board. Making remote idle supervision
  work needs a changed-content signal from board-server at its own mutation
  boundary; until that exists the judgement is not made rather than guessed.

#### Spend caps are not the control

`--budget` / `--max-budget-usd` keeps working and is **never defaulted from
config** — there is no config key that can seed one — but it is deprecated as a
control and warns once when used. Spawned agents are bounded by prompt quality
and, when a repository arms one, by the supervision deadline above.

`session_manager.providers.codex.goal_token_budget` is a positive integer with
an omitted-setting default of `50000000`. Every active native Codex goal uses
this token budget, for both primary and run owners, unless the board explicitly
sets `budget.kind=none`. An explicit `budget.kind=tokens` overrides the configured
default with the board goal's limit; `budget.kind=none` omits native `tokenBudget`
and logs that choice. Invalid settings refuse rather than falling back to the
default. This token-budget setting is separate from the USD flags above.

Detection of the runtime's `budgetLimited` status is unchanged and still
produces `GoalRunSignalBudgetLimited`, its recovery behaviour, and its
`spawn status` visibility. It is deliberately kept **out** of `providerlimits`:
that budget is the one this runtime sent, so it reports exhaustion of a cap we
imposed on one run and proves nothing about the shared subscription. Letting it
suppress a group would let a single small-budget run block every unrelated spawn
on that pool.

`session_manager.providers.claude.goal_ack_timeout` is a positive Go duration
string with an omitted-setting default of `30s`. The budget begins when the
Claude provider process is launched, not when the later scanner waiter is
created; pre-launch preparation consumes none of it, while provider boot and
render lag do. Zero, negative, null, malformed, and unreadable settings refuse
startup rather than silently falling back.

### Per-role model preferences

A ceiling and a default answer different questions, and configuring one does not
give you the other.

- A **ceiling** (`model`/`model_criterion`/`reasoning_effort` in v2,
  `allowed_models` or `entries` plus `resolution_model` in v3) bounds a selection the caller has
  already made. `task-board spawn` refuses an empty `--model` before any ceiling
  is consulted, so a ceiling on its own never produces a selection. Even
  `model_criterion: "equal"`, the tightest v2 bound, only rewrites a model that
  was typed out in full.
- A **default** (`default_model`/`default_reasoning_effort`) supplies the
  selection the caller omitted. It exists only under a `roles.<role>` key,
  because a runtime-wide default would silently decide the selection for every
  role that never opted in, and it is never merged into the effective policy
  other roles inherit.

Both keys are accepted under `spawn-policy-v2` and `spawn-policy-v3` with the
same meaning.

Rules the runtime enforces:

1. An explicit flag always wins. A default seeds only the dimension that was
   omitted, per dimension, so `--model X` with no `--reasoning-effort` still
   picks up the role's default effort.
2. A seeded pair is then resolved by exactly the same ceiling pipeline an
   explicit pair goes through, so a default can never widen a role's bounds.
3. A default that its own role ceiling would adjust is refused with
   `spawn_role_default_outside_ceiling`. Silently clamping it would let the
   config name one model while every spawn of the role quietly ran another.
4. `default_reasoning_effort` is refused for Gemini and Agy roles, which have
   no effort dimension (Antigravity encodes effort in the model ID), and a
   default effort the default model does not support is refused with
   `invalid_spawn_role_default`. That second refusal is what currently rejects
   every Muse role default: the runtime owns a `high`/`xhigh`/`max`
   vocabulary, but each registered muse model declares none
   ([Muse readiness](../.specs/spawn-model-selection.md#muse-readiness)).
5. A default removes manual **model selection**. It does not waive the
   deliberate-selection gate: under `spawn-policy-v2` an ordered
   `model_criterion` still demands `--selection-rationale` on every spawn. A role
   that must run fully unattended needs `spawn-policy-v3` with
   `adjustment_confirmation: "none"`.

Verify a role without launching an agent:

```bash
task-board q 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=context-builder, agent=codex)'
```

`resolved_role_default.spawns_without_manual_selection` is `true` only when the
seed alone forms a launchable selection for that runtime, and
`resolved_role_default.provenance` names the exact config keys it came from.

#### Tuning another role

Per-role policy is keyed on the role name alone; it does not read the role's
prompt contract. A ceiling and its defaults may therefore be configured before
`.roles/<role>/` exists, and the preflight above will answer for that role
immediately. Nothing can be spawned as the role until the contract lands, so
such an entry is deliberate forward-looking config, not live policy — say so
wherever it is introduced.

This repository's own `context-builder` entry is exactly that case. Context
building is an extraction job, so the owner's intent is that it runs on the
cheapest admitted tier of either runtime rather than inheriting the default
`gpt-5.6-sol` / `claude-fable-5` bounds:

```jsonc
"ceilings": {
  "codex": {
    "model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "model_criterion": "less_or_equal", "reasoning_effort": "high",
    "roles": {
      "context-builder": {
        "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "model_criterion": "less_or_equal", "reasoning_effort": "high",
        "default_model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "default_reasoning_effort": "high"
      }
    }
  },
  "claude": {
    "model": "claude-fable-5", "model_criterion": "less_or_equal",
    "roles": {
      "context-builder": {
        "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "model_criterion": "less_or_equal",
        "default_model": "claude-sonnet-5"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The `.roles/context-builder/` prompt contract is owned by
`TASK-260728-3u5ufs`, which also carries the live repository-level acceptance
proof that a real spawn of the role resolves to these pairs with no `--model`.
Until then the entry is verified by preflight only, which is why the two are
linked on the board.

To tune any other role, copy the shape: put the bound and the seed under the
same `roles.<role>` key for each runtime the role may run on, keep the seed
inside its own bound, and confirm with the preflight query above before relying
on it. Configuring a role that will never be spawned is dead config — pair it
with the task that introduces the role.

## Feature registry

`features` and `remote.features` are closed registries.

| Key | Type | Required | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `features.agent_context.enabled` | boolean | Yes when object is present | Feature defaults to `true` when section is absent | Real prompt/query/response shaping kill switch. |
| `features.agent_context.description` | non-empty string | No | Canonical built-in text | Operator-facing description. |
| `features.agent_context.profile` | `"full"` or `"lite"` | No | `"full"` | Base representation. |
| `features.agent_context.prompt` | object | No | Profile defaults | Prompt section detail. |
| `features.agent_context.queries` | object | No | Profile defaults | Query response detail. |
| `features.agent_context.responses` | object | No | Profile defaults | Spawn/observe response detail. |
| `features.context_sharing.enabled` | boolean | Yes when object is present | Feature defaults to `false` when section is absent | Declared gate; runtime remains inactive until implemented. |
| `features.context_sharing.description` | non-empty string | No | Canonical built-in text | Operator-facing description. |
| `features.primary_session_goals.enabled` | boolean | Yes when object is present | Built-in goal-plane default | Gates parent/primary goal mutation and Session Manager binding. |
| `features.primary_session_goals.description` | non-empty string | No | Canonical built-in text | Operator-facing goal-plane contract. |
| `features.spawned_run_goals.enabled` | boolean | Yes when object is present | Built-in goal-plane default | Gates goal-bound spawn launches and run-goal mutation. |
| `features.spawned_run_goals.description` | non-empty string | No | Canonical built-in text | Operator-facing goal-plane contract. |

Agent-context nested keys:

- `prompt.role`: `"full"` or `"contract"`; `prompt.dod`: `"full"` or
  `"deduplicated"`; `prompt.skills`: `"full"` or `"catalog"`;
  `prompt.cli_reference`: `"full"` or `"task-specific"`; `prompt.safety`:
  `"full"` or `"compact"`.
- `prompt.preconditions.mode`: `"inline"` or `"budgeted"`.
- `prompt.preconditions.inline_bytes`: positive integer.
- `queries.project_config`: `"full"` or `"spawn-preflight"`; `queries.schema`:
  `"full"` or `"on-error"`; `queries.summary`: `"full"` or `"routing"`;
  `queries.plan`: `"full"` or `"compact"`.
- `responses.spawn`: `"full"` or `"compact"`; `responses.observation`:
  `"full"` or `"terminal-delta"`; `responses.selection`: `"full"` or
  `"delta-only"`.

The compatibility-only root/remote `agent_context` object has the same nested
keys except `enabled` and `description`.

## Context retention and GC

`contexts.retention` tunes when a context stops being kept. Every knob has a
documented default and an unset knob keeps it, so a project that names one
window does not accidentally zero the other eight.

Durations accept Go's spelling (`90m`, `6h`, `720h`) plus a day suffix (`30d`,
`0.5d`). A zero, negative, or unparseable value is refused rather than
defaulted, and so is an unrecognized key: a misspelled `ready_tll` that fell
through to the default would read as configured while nothing changed.

| Key | Type | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `ready_ttl` | duration | `30d` | How long a published base stays selectable, measured from publication. |
| `stale_ttl` | duration | `7d` | How long an invalidated snapshot is kept for inspection. A stale record is the only place the evidence of a drift lives. |
| `building_ttl` | duration | `6h` | When an abandoned provisional record is retired. A run still holding the writer lease is slow, not abandoned, and is never taken. |
| `retired_ttl` | duration | `24h` | Grace period before a retired context's local provider handle may be released, and before its Claude-side artifacts may be reclaimed. |
| `unpublished_materialization_ttl` | duration | `24h` | How long a detached successful session that was never published may be reattached. Also bounds an orphan left by a failed, cancelled, or rerouted run. It does not govern a handle that published and was detached for another reason — that is an idle eviction. |
| `materialization_idle_ttl` | duration | `24h` | When a local handle that is not a fork source is dropped, together with the provider files it named — an unused ready handle, or one detached because its exact target element closed. The registry record survives untouched; a later run that ranks that base finds no forkable local handle, drops it from its own selection and starts cold. So this costs one run's exploration, never a lineage. |
| `payload_ttl` | duration | `7d` | How long retired payload bytes may remain in the cross-machine payload store. |
| `max_ready_per_binding` | positive integer | `3` | Ready snapshots kept per selection group — the tuple the resolver actually ranks within: provider, binding scope and element, producer role. |
| `max_descendants_per_base` | positive integer | `64` | Ready descendants kept per base. |

```json
{
  "contexts": {
    "retention": {
      "ready_ttl": "14d",
      "stale_ttl": "72h",
      "max_ready_per_binding": 5
    }
  }
}
```

Notes:

- **Retention is set by staleness and secret exposure, not by disk.** The
  measured economics (`TASK-260728-1wpsph` §9.2) put storage four to five orders
  of magnitude below the token saving: one warm fork pays for roughly eleven
  thousand months of that context's storage, and a hundred-thousand-context
  fleet costs under a dollar a month. Shortening `ready_ttl` to save space is
  not a trade that exists.
- The caps are enforced **at sweep time, never at publication**. A run that has
  already been paid for is always recorded, and the surplus is trimmed
  afterwards by retiring the least-recently-selected oldest eligible leaf, in
  `(last_selected_at, ready_at, CTX-ID)` order. Rejecting a publication because
  a group was full would throw away the expensive half of the transaction.
- A cap never retires an ancestor a retained ready snapshot still inherits
  from, and never a pinned or leased record. When those leave a cap unmet it is
  met partially and the holds are reported, so an operator can see which
  snapshot to unpin or which run to wait for.
- Pinning a context exempts it from age and cap pressure and from nothing else.
  It does not bypass freshness, secret policy, or close-driven retirement.
- The sweep runs through the Session Manager — `task-board session
  sweep-contexts [--dry-run]` — because it needs both planes. The board decides
  what is past its window or over a cap; the machine holding the provider
  handles is the only one that can say what a live run has leased, and the only
  one that can release anything. A sweep that could not observe leases plans
  nothing and says so, rather than treating unknown lease state as unleased.
- Payload bytes are withdrawn only for records the registry already holds as
  retired, and provider-local artifacts are reclaimed only after
  `retired_ttl`. Deletion never runs ahead of retirement.
- Releasing a local handle deletes the provider files it named first. The
  private catalog is the only thing on a machine that can turn a CTX id into a
  transcript, so dropping the entry while the bytes are still there would make
  them unnameable and permanent. A handle whose bytes cannot be proved gone is
  kept and reported instead.
- `--dry-run` shows the destructive half. Every planned handle release reports
  the provider file count the applying pass would delete, and a handle the
  applying pass would refuse to release is reported as `kept` rather than folded
  into `planned`.
- A local eviction is never a board-wide finding. Dropping this machine's copy
  of a `ready` context leaves the registry record exactly as it was — the
  snapshot is intact on whichever machine still holds it — and a run here that
  ranks it records a `context_not_materialized` rejection and starts cold. An
  explicitly named `--context` is refused instead, because an operator who named
  a base is owed the refusal rather than a silent cold start.

## Context security

`contexts` owns the decisions a project makes about shared agent contexts. The
two security ones are fail-closed: an absent section means no role receives restricted
snapshots and no board-bound project context can be published at all.

| Key | Type | Required | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `clearance` | object keyed by role or `null` | No | None | Grants a role the secret classes it may receive. |
| `clearance.<role>.allowed_secret_classes` | array of `"unrestricted"` / `"restricted"` | Required inside a clearance entry | None | The classes that role may consume; a role that is not listed is cleared for `unrestricted` only. |
| `builder_allowlist` | array of repo-relative path specs or `null` | No | None | The read scope a `context-builder` run is confined to. Entries are directory prefixes or globs; absolute paths and `..` are rejected. |
| `payloads` | object or `null` | No | None (off) | Cross-machine payload transport. An absent section means no payload is transportable by any route. |
| `payloads.mode` | `"off"` / `"unrestricted"` | No | `"off"` | `unrestricted` permits transporting payloads for contexts classified `unrestricted`; restricted payloads never leave their machine. Any other value is refused rather than defaulted. |
| `retention` | object or `null` | No | Every knob at its documented default | Retention and GC tuning. Unlike the security keys this one is not fail-closed, because its absence means "keep the shipped policy" rather than "grant nothing". |

```json
{
  "contexts": {
    "clearance": {
      "reviewer": {"allowed_secret_classes": ["unrestricted", "restricted"]}
    },
    "builder_allowlist": ["pkg", "tools", "docs", "README.md"],
    "payloads": {"mode": "off"}
  }
}
```

Notes:

- A snapshot is classified once, at publication, by a scan of the completed
  provider transcript. A rule match or a scan that could not read part of the
  transcript classifies `restricted`; only a complete, clean scan classifies
  `unrestricted`. An operational scan failure refuses the publication rather
  than assigning a class.
- The classification is immutable. If a published snapshot turns out to be
  misclassified, its payload is quarantined, the bytes are deleted through the
  board's payload store, and the context is retired with
  `security_reclassification_required`; the label is never rewritten, because
  whoever already fetched the bytes still has them.
- A deletion that fails does not block the retirement, but it is never lost
  either. The outstanding withdrawal is recorded with a typed failure code and
  `retry_context_payload_withdrawal(CTX-…)` converges it. The code is an enum
  rather than the payload store's own message: an error authored by the
  component holding the secret bytes must not be persisted where a board Viewer
  can read it, and truncating it would not help, since a short credential
  survives truncation intact.
- A board that has no payload store bound refuses to record an available payload
  at all, with `context_payload_capability_required`. Bytes that could never be
  withdrawn are never published in the first place.
- `payloads.mode` is off unless a project writes it, and off means off in every
  direction: an upload is refused before the body is read, a published payload is
  not served, and selection plans a build rather than a fetch. Enabling it is a
  project decision *and* a deployment one — the board-server reads the same
  `contexts.payloads.mode` key (or `BOARD_CONTEXT_PAYLOAD_MODE`) and advertises
  `context_payload=resource-v1` only when it has both the mode and a bound
  object store. A missing payload capability is metadata-only operation, never a
  registry failure.
- An upload is screened on its own content, not on the record's label: the
  classifier runs over the exact bytes and refuses anything short of a clean
  `unrestricted` verdict, so a transcript carrying credentials is refused even
  when the context it belongs to was published `unrestricted`.
- `builder_allowlist` is a project key and stays one. board-server has no
  equivalent and does not decide confinement: it never sees a project's
  `task-board.config.json`, and one deployment serves many boards, so a
  server-side copy would be a second definition of the read scope that agrees
  until it does not. The rule is enforced by the two components holding the
  config — the producing machine refuses to upload a project base whose declared
  reads escape the allowlist, before the bytes leave it, and the consuming
  Session Manager refuses to materialize one after they arrive. An unconfigured
  allowlist denies every project base on both sides.
- Board access is not clearance. A board Editor can mutate registry metadata and
  still be refused a restricted context, because the pre-materialization gate
  decides from role policy alone.
- Only a `context-builder` run under `builder_allowlist` may publish a
  board-bound project context, and its declared ingestion path specs must stay
  inside the allowlist. Role and task contexts are published by ordinary roles
  under register-time classification and are unaffected.
- The allowlist is enforced at the launch, and the claim is sealed there too.
  When the Session Manager materializes a `context-builder` run it first refuses
  one whose declared ingestion scope already reaches outside the allowlist or
  when no confiner is registered; it seals nothing at that planning step. At the
  real provider launch, both Codex and Claude host factories install the
  confinement and the successfully launched host reports the mechanism and
  roots it enforced. The manager checks that reported scope against this
  allowlist and against the run's declared specs — a boundary that confined a
  wider scope than the project permits, or that left a declared spec unconfined,
  produces no claim — and only then seals a signed claim naming the CTX, the observed
  revision, the producing run, the producer role and builder profile, a digest
  of the enforced allowlist, a digest of the ingestion scope, the mechanism, and
  a digest of the scope actually confined. Only that claim crosses the wire; the
  paths stay in this file.
- `tb-sessiond` registers the provider-host confiner for both Codex and Claude.
  On macOS each adapter wraps its real provider process in the
  `darwin-seatbelt-project-read-v2` profile. Reads are denied globally and
  restored only for immutable system runtime paths, provider-private runtime
  state and its dedicated temp directory, and configured repo-relative roots.
  User-home reads remain denied outside those explicit runtime exceptions.
  Writes to the whole project are denied, preventing rename/link/copy/clone
  escapes that could otherwise move protected bytes into an allowed root. A
  manager without the registration, a provider host that cannot report the
  installed boundary, or a platform without a supported mechanism is refused
  with `context_builder_confinement_unavailable` and seals nothing. That is the
  fail-closed direction: a project context is inherited by every later run, so
  an unconfined launch cannot become a reusable base.
- Publication presents the sealed claim and never produces one. That ordering is
  the rule: a publisher runs after the transcript already exists, so anything it
  signed at that point would attest to a confinement that either happened or did
  not, and the signature would say nothing either way. A project base published
  without a confined launch has no claim to present and is refused with
  `context_builder_allowlist_denied`. A claim that names no mechanism or reports
  no confined scope is refused with `context_builder_confinement_unavailable`,
  because a comparison of the record against this file is not evidence that a
  process was restricted.
- The claim is signed, not merely hashed, because this file is checked into the
  repository. Anyone who can read the repo can compute the correct allowlist
  digest, so a digest alone would prove nothing about who confined the builder.
  The signature is an HMAC under a secret the Session Manager generates on
  first use and keeps `0600` in its own private state directory
  (`enforcement.key`, beside the control token). Sealed claims live beside it in
  `builder-enforcement/`. Neither is ever sent anywhere: only the signature and
  a public fingerprint of the key leave the machine.
- Consequences an operator should expect. A project base published on one
  machine verifies only on that machine; another machine refuses it with
  `context_builder_allowlist_denied` and builds its own context instead, which
  is the documented fallback rather than an error to work around. Deleting
  `enforcement.key` invalidates every project base this machine published — they
  are retired and rebuilt, not relabelled. Role and task contexts are unaffected
  by all of this and need no key.

## Session and version-control sections

Current Session Manager path:
`session_manager.providers.codex.auto_continue`. Compatibility path:
`codex.session.auto_continue`.

> The "provider" here is the Session Manager host — Codex or Claude, a binary set —
> not a `spawn.runtimes` entry. Renaming this axis is design task 14's scope, not
> this task's.

| Key | Type | Required | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `enabled` | boolean | No | `false` | Enables continuation nudges. |
| `delay_seconds` | integer | No | `20` | Delay before a nudge. |
| `max_nudges_per_turn` | integer | No | `2` | Per-turn nudge cap. |
| `prompt` | string | No | Built-in continuation prompt | Nudge text. |
| `version_control.confirm` | boolean | No | `false` | Enables scoped commit acknowledgement. |
| `version_control.desired_commit_time` | non-empty string | Required when `confirm=true` | None | Owner timing message shown at the gate. Never parsed. |
| `version_control.commit_time_policy` | object or `null` | No | `null` (absent) | Computable commit-time policy. Shapes: `{"kind": "now"}` resolves to the landing instant; `{"kind": "previous_day_at", "time": "HH:MM", "timezone": "<IANA zone>"}` resolves to the calendar day before the landing's wall-clock date in that zone, at that wall-clock time, with that zone's offset on that date, formatted RFC3339. Unknown kinds, malformed `time`, unknown zones, and `previous_day_at` without `time`/`timezone` are refused; a duplicated key is refused by the file-level duplicate-key scan. |
| `version_control.commit_time_policy.kind` | string | Yes, when the policy is present | None | One of `now`, `previous_day_at`. |
| `version_control.commit_time_policy.time` | `HH:MM` 24-hour string | Required when `kind` is `previous_day_at` | None | Wall-clock time on the previous calendar day. |
| `version_control.commit_time_policy.timezone` | IANA time zone string | Required when `kind` is `previous_day_at` | None | Zone whose calendar and offset fix the resolved instant. |

`worktree integrate` and `worktree complete` resolve the commit time in this
order: an explicit `--commit-time` flag first (the manual resume path); else
`commit_time_policy` when configured; else, when `confirm=false`, the current
time; else they refuse `commit_time_policy_unconfigured` before the
transaction opens, naming `version_control.commit_time_policy` in the packet.
The tool never asks and never guesses. The runner-owned landing of a
story_final integration run re-enters `worktree integrate` without the flag,
so a project with `confirm=true` needs a policy for that landing to proceed
(issue #355). `worktree checkpoint` does not consult the policy yet
(TASK-260903-2e8rws). The resolved time and its source (`flag`,
`policy`, `now`) are recorded in the integration transaction and in the
`integrate --json` result.

Managed Story commit signing is not a configurable feature and is independent
of `version_control.confirm`. `worktree checkpoint` and `worktree integrate`
explicitly pass `-S` for checkpoint, revalidation materialization, final Story,
and board-only commits. Git resolves `user.name`, `user.email`,
`user.signingKey`, and its signing backend from repository configuration. A
missing or failing signer refuses without an unsigned fallback; setup never
creates a key or substitutes an identity.

## Repair workflow

1. Read the refusal's file path, dotted key, found value, and expected value.
2. Open that exact file plus this reference.
3. Edit only the rejected project file; do not rerun setup hoping for repair.
4. Re-run the original command. Task-board rereads the file and either accepts
   the whole shape or reports the next exact refusal.

### Clearing provider-limit groups

`task-board limits clear --group codex-plan` resolves the active provider identity.
Use `--identity <indexed-key>` to select a specific indexed identity. In addition to
configured table groups, clear accepts exact `codex-unmapped:<model>` names already
present in that selected Codex identity's state, including historical model names.
For example, a stored `codex-unmapped:gpt-6-astra` row can be cleared with:

```bash
task-board limits clear --group codex-unmapped:gpt-6-astra
task-board limits clear --group codex-unmapped:gpt-6-astra --identity <indexed-key>
```

The prefix alone does not establish membership. Unknown groups or identities,
provider mismatches and indeterminate state reads refuse without writing state or
index files (`provider_limit_group_unknown`, `provider_limit_identity_unknown`,
`provider_limit_identity_provider_mismatch`, `provider_limit_state_indeterminate`).
An absent or available table-backed group remains ungated and unchanged. Clearing
a suppressed or probing group retains a `probe_eligible` record at backoff step 0,
erases the held lease and reports its displaced run and lease timestamps. The next
spawn must still win the atomic probe claim; clear does not grant unrestricted
availability.

A workload preflight digest binds ordinary availability and probe claimability
separately. Changing a group between probe-eligible and probing invalidates the
old digest even though neither state is ordinarily available. Spawn admits exact
membership in the snapshot's available or claimable pairs, then enforces the live
atomic claim; a dropped diagnostic by itself never authorizes a pair.
# Resources

Resources are owned by the board element they are attached to. Use explicit
CRUD operations against that element ID. Local payloads are grouped under
`.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/`; remote board-server uses the equivalent
element-scoped filesystem or MinIO object-key prefix.

## Resource Types

| Type | Purpose | Example |
|------|---------|---------|
| **precondition** | Inputs needed before work starts | Design docs, specs, instructions, API schemas |
| **outcome** | Results produced by work | Research findings, implementation notes, analysis |

## Commands

```bash
# Create a resource
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_design.md, type=precondition, content="...", description="Architecture design")'
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content="...", description="Research findings")'

# Replace an existing resource
task-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md, type=outcome, content="revised findings", description="Research findings v2")'
task-board resource update TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_results.md --name reports/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome

# Adopt an existing orphaned local payload without rewriting it
task-board resource adopt TASK-260218-abc revisions/2/TASK-260218-abc_results.md --type outcome --description "Recovered result"

# Delete a resource (destructive — requires --confirm)
task-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md)'
task-board resource delete TASK-260218-abc artifacts/obsolete/TASK-260218-abc_design.md --confirm

# View resources on an element
task-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { preconditionResources outcomeResources }'
```

## Element-Owned Storage

Resources from different tasks are not stored in one shared file pile. Each
element gets its own directory, and every resource attached to that element is
stored below it:

```
.task-board/.resources/
├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/
│   ├── findings/
│   │   └── investigation.md
│   ├── artifacts/
│   │   └── run-1/
│   │       └── output.json
│   ├── reports/
│   │   └── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md
│   └── revisions/
│       └── 2/
│           └── review.md
└── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/
    └── research.md         # outcome
```

The resource `name` is a safe forward-slash-relative path. Agents may choose
the hierarchy that fits the artifact and nest it further as needed. Every
segment must be non-empty printable UTF-8 and must not be `.`, `..`, an
absolute/drive-qualified path, or contain a backslash. Raw forward slashes are
the only hierarchy delimiter; malformed URL escapes and encoded or repeatedly
encoded separators/traversal are rejected. Attach related files to the same
owning element; do not invent a generic board-wide resource task or write
payloads into `.task-board/.resources/` manually.

Directories are structural nodes, not payloads. Resource CRUD, validation, and
handoff evidence recognize regular files only; a directory named like a report
cannot be adopted or used to satisfy outcome evidence.

Local and board-server filesystem element deletion is journaled and
crash-recoverable. Direct resource CRUD first resolves any pending staged
element delete under the board mutation lock. MinIO element/subtree deletion
currently fails before mutation with the typed
`ErrAtomicResourceDeleteUnsupported` capability error; ordinary resource CRUD
remains available.

References are tracked in `progress.md` as markdown links:
```markdown
## Precondition Resources
- [specs/auth/design.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/precondition/specs/auth/design.md) — Architecture design

## Outcome Resources
- [reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md](board-resource://TASK-260203-g7h8i9/outcome/reports/TASK-260203-g7h8i9_results.md) — Research findings
```

## Spawn Integration

Precondition resources are automatically injected into agent prompts by `task-board spawn`. Full prompts preserve normal text inlining. Lite prompts use the resolved `agent_context.prompt.preconditions` policy: by default, text below `inline_bytes` is rendered inline, while larger text and binary resources are written read-only under `.temp/resources/` and represented by their name, description, byte count, SHA-256 digest, and local path. This means:

1. Attach instructions/specs as precondition resources to a task
2. Run `task-board spawn TASK-XX --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` — agent gets everything automatically
3. Agent saves results via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write, then `update_resource(...)` for revisions

## Best Practices

- **Use preconditions for agent instructions** — attach design docs, specs, or step-by-step instructions before spawning
- **Use outcomes for work products** — research findings, analysis, implementation notes
- **Attach to the owning element** — pass the Task, Bug, Story, or Epic ID that owns the artifact; the storage layer groups its payloads under that element directory automatically
- **Use explicit create/update/delete** — `add_resource` / `resource add` must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` when replacing an existing payload
- **Use adopt only for local repair** — `resource adopt` registers bytes already stored at `.resources/<elementID>/<relative-resource-path>` without rewriting them; a missing payload returns resource-not-found and an existing declaration returns resource-exists
- **Choose a useful hierarchy inside the owning element** — `findings/`, `artifacts/`, `reports/`, and `revisions/` are conventions, not a fixed schema; nest further when it makes the handoff easier to navigate
- **Use safe relative resource paths** — `/` separates validated path segments; empty, dot/traversal, absolute/drive-qualified, backslash, control-character, malformed escape, and encoded/repeatedly encoded separator or traversal forms are rejected in local and remote storage
- **Attach files, not folders** — directories only organize resource files and never satisfy resource existence or handoff evidence
- **Always use board commands** — never create/edit resource files or progress.md resource sections manually
- **Name handoff evidence with task scope** — keep the task ID in the resource path, for example `reports/TASK-260218-abc_architecture-design.md`, so `task-board handoff` can recognize it
- **Add descriptions** — the `description=` param adds a one-line description shown in spawn prompts

---

# Bugs

Bugs are elements of the board, living inside stories alongside tasks.

```bash
# Create bug
task-board m 'create(type=bug, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=crash-on-start, description="App crashes when starting recording")'

# Track like any element
task-board m 'set_status(BUG-260218-abc, status=development)'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Reproduce the issue")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Find root cause")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Write fix")'
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(BUG-260218-abc, text="Add regression test")'
```
# Negative Evidence Contract

A passing suite means nothing unless something in it would have failed. This
file is the single source of truth for the rules that make a green check mean
something, and for the incident record behind each one. The operative wording
that reaches every spawned child lives in the spawn template section
`Evidence That Counts`; the reviewer's licence lives in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`;
the repeated-variant heuristic lives in `.roles/orchestrator/role.md`. Read this
file when you need the shapes, the failure vocabulary, or the incidents that
produced a rule — do not restate these rules in other documents.

| Rule | Operative statement | Evidence record |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Negative tests for gates | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Narrowing, not deleting | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Production call site | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Absence vs failure to read | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Prove, or report nothing | spawn template, `Evidence That Counts` | this file |
| Reviewer licence to defeat a gate | `.roles/reviewer/role.md` | this file |
| Many variants, one unowned decision | `.roles/orchestrator/role.md` | this file |

## Negative Tests For Gates

Any feature that GATES, REFUSES, VALIDATES, AUTHORIZES, or ATTESTS must ship
with negative tests that fail when the gate admits what it is supposed to
reject. A positive test proving the happy path passes is not evidence that the
gate works; it is evidence that the gate is reachable.

This is the dominant defect class in this repository's 2026-07-28..08-03 waves.
Every instance below was found because a reviewer deliberately attacked the
gate, not because anyone read the code:

- the fact-assay attested a COLD child that never inherited the context, because
  it checked inventory, session id, digests, and output shape, but never
  inheritance;
- the assay fork had repository tool access, so it could answer from disk while
  the base was false;
- the builder attestation accepted a correctly-shaped forged digest that the
  caller minted itself;
- clearance enforcement existed but no production path called it, and counting
  tests around a fake proved nothing;
- an unknown assay event preserved a false zero-tool observation;
- a provider capability was declared absent to disable a flaky check, and the
  claim did not reproduce (9/11 success).

## Standard Negative Shapes

Use these names in checklists, verdicts, and test names. They are the shapes a
negative test must cover and the shapes a reviewer looks for first.

| Shape | What it looks like when nobody tests for it | What the negative test must do |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Forged or self-minted evidence | The caller supplies the proof that the gate checks, and a correctly-shaped value passes. | Mint the evidence in the test the way a caller could, and require refusal. |
| Absent evidence treated as satisfied | Missing input takes the permissive branch; the suite only ever supplies the input. | Remove the evidence entirely and require refusal, not a default-pass. |
| Check present but uncalled from production | The guard is implemented and unit-tested by direct call; no production path invokes it. | Drive the real entry point — launch, materialize, resolve, publish — and name the production call site. |
| Bypass path around the check | One entry point enforces; a second, older, or retry/renewal path does not. | Exercise every entry point that reaches the protected state, including retry, renewal, resume, and recovery, and every delivery surface that carries the rule — a contract clause reaching one context profile and not the other is this shape. |
| Capability claim that does not reproduce | A capability is declared absent or present to make a flaky check go away. | Re-run the claim enough times to state a real rate, or report unknown. |

## Derive Completeness From An Independent Source

Completeness is derived from the pinned source or generated artifact, never
enumerated by hand. When a registry, schema set, or declared scope defines the
members, derive the expected set from that source and fail when any member lacks
enforcement. This rule comes from five consecutive review rounds that each
closed exactly the hand-picked members named by the previous reviewer; the next
unlisted member defeated every list.

A completeness test that reads the implementation's own table is circular and
can never detect a missing entry: removing the entry also removes it from the
test's expected set. Derive the expectation from the normative source of truth,
not from the code under test. This distinction was what let the next Story's
round-1 review demand a source-derived completeness test instead of accepting
the producer's two selected cases.

## Prove Exact Bounds At The Production Entry

Prove every bound in both directions at the production entry: accept at the
limit and refuse past the limit. Character bounds count characters rather than
bytes. The motivating failure was a value containing 3,999 characters but
5,999 bytes that production rejected with a byte comparison even though the
normative `string[1..4096]` bound permitted it; helper-only or ASCII-only tests
could not expose that narrowing.

## Disable Every Refusal Clause

Disable each refusal clause individually. If the suite stays green, that clause
is a hole unless the test or evidence documents it as provably subsumed and
names the check that subsumes it. This is measured rather than hypothetical: in
one file, disabling 110 clause sites produced 45 surviving mutants, and 12 of
those survivors were genuine widenings.

Numeric-bound mutation and clause-disablement are different classes, and one
harness rarely generates both. Numeric mutation finds off-by-one defects but
cannot express disabling a tagged-union arm, cross-field equality, or grammar
predicate; clause disablement finds those omissions but cannot move a numeric
boundary. The 110-site clause run exposed widenings that the numeric harness
could not generate, so run both classes.

## Check Refusals Against The Normative Source

A green test can defend a bug when it asserts a refusal that the normative
source does not require. One observed test required production to reject values
that the pinned specification permitted, so the suite protected the narrowing
instead of detecting it. Whenever a test encodes a refusal, check that refusal
against the pinned source rather than copying current behaviour into the
assertion.

## Eliminate Ambient Test Inputs

No test may depend on ambient environment. Fixtures set explicit identity and
all other values that could otherwise come from global configuration, the OS,
or developer-machine state. A developer machine let Git derive an author name
from the OS and passed the suite, while a CI runner with an empty GECOS field
failed with `empty ident name`; only CI can catch this class, so the fixture
must make its identity deterministic before CI exercises it.

## Prove A Bound By Narrowing, Not By Deleting

A test that removes a gate entirely proves only that the gate exists. It says
nothing about whether the gate covers the right class. Restrict the gate to a
narrower condition instead and require a named test to fail.

Two repository incidents where a narrowed gate survived a green suite:

- **BUG-260801-3kiloe F1** — the recovery successor cap was proven only by total
  removal. Narrowing it to provider-limit signals left every other recoverable
  failure unbounded, with the whole suite still green.
- **TASK-260802-17wetr F16** — thirteen cycles later, the same shape: the
  probe-lease cap survived deletion tests while the renewal path escaped it.
- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 1** — this contract itself. The reviewer clauses
  landed in the role body and the shipped test checked the role file and the
  lite template separately, so both halves were green while the composed lite
  prompt carried neither. Assert on the composed production artifact, not on the
  pieces it is built from.
- **TASK-260730-2c1e8l cycle 2** — the same contract, one level up, and the
  clearest statement of the difference between the two shapes. The cycle-1 fix
  did assert on the composed prompt, but it called the composition helper
  directly and handed it the role, task-class filter, and profile itself.
  Production builds those inputs inside the spawn entry point, so a mutant
  narrowing only the real lite-reviewer routing just before composition left the
  test green while an actual lite reviewer lost its gate-defeat licence. A test
  that supplies the inputs is testing the helper, not the path. Obtain the
  artifact the way the consumer does — here, read the prompt file recorded on
  the run manifest after driving the entry point — so role resolution, the
  task-class filter, and profile resolution are all under test rather than
  assumed.

Three traps when running the mutants themselves, all hit while proving this file:

- **The mutant must be re-run with `-count=1`.** `go test` caches on package
  sources; a test that reads `.roles/*.md` or any file outside its package at
  runtime returns a stale cached PASS after that file is mutated. A cached green
  is not a surviving mutant, it is an unexecuted test.
- **A passing assertion may be satisfied by the wrong source.** Two mutants of
  the producer DoD entries survived because the phrase asserted also appears,
  differently worded, in the shared spawn template. Choose assertion wording
  that only the intended source can supply, then narrow that source and require
  the named test to fail.
- **Restore the mutant from a copy, not from git.** `git checkout <file>`
  restores from the index, which silently discards uncommitted work in that file
  — during this task it reverted the whole cycle-2 change, not just the mutant.
  Copy the file aside before mutating, restore from the copy, and verify the
  restoration by diffing against it before rerunning the gates.

## A Gate Publishes Its Coverage As A Ratio

Prose about what a gate checks cannot distinguish 7 of 7 from 3 of 7. Every
statement such a description makes is usually true — it is true of what the gate
can see, and silent about what it cannot. The longer and more careful the
description, the more thorough the gate looks.

Report the fraction of intended targets the gate actually reaches, and assert
that fraction so it reddens when a target drops out of reach. A target no mutant
can redden is not covered, however elaborate the structure around it.

Three defects of this exact shape, each found only once a ratio existed:

- an AST call graph that recorded an edge only for a direct identifier call
  covered 3 of 7 guarded functions while every pin passed; the package's real
  dispatch was a function-value table, and the blind spot was invisible from
  inside;
- a classifier keyed on prose that a sibling gate was actively rewriting silently
  dropped 8 rows, taking a suite from 107 executed subtests to 66, while its
  completeness check `count == 0` reported nothing, because four is not zero;
- an ownership gate that proved a symbol exists and a case is linked reported 48
  sections as owned; measured against the specification, those bindings
  discharged 2 of 394 normative clauses.

A non-empty check on a derived set is the recurring form. `len(x) > 0` answers a
different question from `len(x) == expected`, and only the second notices a
narrowing.

## A Blind Spot Is Not An Empty Set

"Nothing is there" and "nothing this checker can see" are different facts, and
only the second is a statement about the checker. Reporting the first when you
mean the second converts a limitation into a clean bill of health, and the reader
has no way to tell.

An ownership gate scanned for uppercase RFC 2119 keywords. Sections stating their
obligations in declarative present tense scored zero, were bucketed as carrying
no obligations, and were admitted with no evidence required. Its documentation
said "there is no obligation to enumerate". The true sentence was "the section
carries no keyword line, so the scanner cannot see its obligations" — and 19 of
151 headings were free-admission surface the moment they were bound, including a
19-row exit-code registry and a closed manifest specification.

Two rules follow:

- State the bound of what the checker can see, next to what it reports. A
  residual class that is named can be attacked later; one that is implied cannot.
- Do not let the bucket for "nothing to check" also be the bucket that skips the
  check. Where absence of measurable obligation is a legitimate state, require an
  explicit reviewed justification for it, so the weakest entries are the ones
  that must say the most rather than the least.

## An Absence And A Failure To Read Are Different Facts

Absence is a legitimate state with a defined fallback. A failed, partial, or
malformed read is evidence of a problem. Treating the second as the first lets
the fallback launder a fault into a normal-looking value, and a fallback defined
for absence must never fire on a read failure.

Each of these was a separate defect in this repository:

- a non-empty value read as verified;
- an unknown scanner version read as clean;
- a partial provenance record read as absent;
- a zero pid start time read as alive;
- a malformed `api_error_status` read as missing;
- an unparsed envelope read as no envelope — this one produced a false provider
  suppression that would have hidden a healthy provider for hours.

## Prove, Or Report Nothing

Where a property cannot be established, report unparsed or unknown rather than
inferring it from a proxy signal. A plausible guess is worse than an explicit
unknown, because callers act on it.

Incident: headless top-level inference in the Claude classifier was unsound in
principle and produced six variants before it was deleted rather than
strengthened.

## Many Variants, One Unowned Decision

A long run of same-shaped variants usually means a decision has more than one
owner, not that there are many bugs. Eleven framing variants stopped reproducing
only when structure got a single scanner and a single state type; every per-site
patch before that produced the next variant. The operative wording is in
`.roles/orchestrator/role.md`: unify the decision, do not patch each site.

## Reviewer Licence

Reviewers are explicitly licensed to attempt to defeat a gate and to reject a
change whose only evidence is positive-path. Grep for callers before believing a
guard is in force. The operative wording is in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`.

## Exact-Evidence Reuse Is Not Full Replay

A green validation result is evidence about ONE candidate: the source and test
tree it ran against, the suite and configuration it ran with, and the
environment it ran in. Progressive checks may reuse that exact result while
all four identities are unchanged; the moment any of them differs, the result
is evidence about a different candidate and a rerun is required. The runtime
binding — which identities are hashed, what `reused_from_revision` records,
and why an absent or malformed environment identity forces a reasoned rerun —
is [docs/delivery-validation.md](../docs/delivery-validation.md). This page
states the shapes a plan and a reviewer check against.

- **Freeze the candidate before execution.** Evidence is bound to a captured
  tree, never to "the worktree as it is now". A result produced while the tree
  was still moving is evidence about no candidate at all.
- **Full replay needs a reason.** Re-running the whole configured suite is
  justified by exactly three things: a changed input identity, coverage the
  reused evidence does not carry, or a recorded diagnostic reason. "To be
  safe", "the runbook always does", or a retry that lost track of what already
  ran are not reasons, and each burns the same budget the incident measured.
- **Reuse never disarms the reviewer.** An independent reviewer retains
  targeted adversarial discretion over a reused green: the attack described in
  `Licence To Defeat A Gate` is against the candidate, not against the log,
  and a reused result is one more input to attack, not a waiver.
- **References and digests replace duplicated corpora.** A manifest of
  content digests proves what was checked; a second copy of the corpus proves
  only that the bytes were attached again. Portable archives are for a named
  consumer that cannot resolve references, and for nothing else.

## Refusal Rows: Research And Validation Budgets

The rows below are what the guidance REFUSES, and the two controls are what it
must keep admitting. Check a research plan, a rework brief, or a validation
decision against both halves: a rule that refuses everything satisfies the
first half and fails the second. Each refusal row names the observed incident
behind it (`TASK-260909-2ax2l1` retrospective and addendum).

| # | Scenario | Verdict | Why, and what the incident looked like |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| R1 | An agent justifies a second serial research prerequisite by consistency alone — "the sibling leaf has a gate, so this class needs one too", "the harness should also cover", symmetry with a prior leaf | **Refused.** A second serial prerequisite needs a checked spec gap (`solution-architect` rule 3: the missing piece, the requirement left incomplete, the spec sections checked) and its own budget; otherwise the finding is a focused regression in the owning leaf | Three nested gate leaves (contract gate, atom shapes, codepoints) were spawned from `repeat-of:` verdicts; production coverage stayed 0/28 vectors, 0/32 guards |
| R2 | Reuse of a green result after the harness, the source tree, the test tree, the configuration or the environment changed | **Refused.** Any changed identity makes the evidence about a different candidate; rerun with the diagnostic reason recorded. An earlier empty candidate never exempts a later source change | Reviewer anchors on a stale producer/revision are documented in two verdicts; a candidate was reviewed against a tree it no longer was |
| R3 | A gate claimed green with logs missing, truncated, unreadable, or ending before the last command | **Refused.** A failed or partial read is not a pass and not an absence; report which commands ran, which exit codes were observed, and mark the rest unrun. A cached `ok` on a package that reads files outside its module is this row | A 20-command suite timed out at command 13 of 20 after twelve greens; the seven unrun commands are unrun, not passed |
| R4 | A retry, resume, recovery or republish that starts its budgets from zero | **Refused.** Worker time, artifact bytes and serial depth accumulate across every continuation of the same leaf; a continuation inherits the budget position it was cancelled at | 13 of 42 runs were cancelled continuations; the chain re-entered full publication validation on an empty-delta research handoff until an operator directive stopped it |
| C1 | An unchanged green candidate: the same frozen tree, suite, configuration and environment, presented again for a later check | **Admissible.** Reuse the exact evidence; do not replay. The reviewer may still attack the candidate | The positive control for R2 and R3: identical identities, readable digest-matching log |
| C2 | A legitimate research-only Change Request with an empty `repository_delta`, whose task-scoped outcomes are attached and whose decision is recorded | **Admissible.** Publish it for independent review, skip source validation as `docs/delivery-validation.md` records, and route to the first implementation slice | The positive control for R1 and R4: one bounded contract task is the intended shape; twelve consecutive empty revisions with no consuming slice is what R1 and R4 refuse |

# Statuses

10 statuses organized in a workflow:

| Status | Meaning | Who sets |
|--------|---------|----------|
| **backlog** | Captured, no work yet | anyone |
| **analysis** | Researching, decomposing | anyone |
| **to-dev** | Ready for development | orchestrator |
| **development** | Writing code | sub-agent |
| **to-review** | Code ready for review | sub-agent |
| **reviewing** | Reviewer child is checking the work | reviewer |
| **integrating** | Accepted Change Request, not landed. Active, never terminal | `accept_cr` only; left only by the integration transaction |
| **done** | Reviewed and accepted leaf work, or computed container completion | integration transaction for Change Request work; reviewer verdict plus the acknowledged transition for inline work; parent aggregation |
| **closed** | Explicit administrative disposition: cancelled, obsolete, or won't do (reason in notes) | anyone |
| **blocked** | Concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision (full evidence packet in notes/resource) | anyone |

## Status Flow

**Happy path:**
```
backlog → analysis → to-dev → development → to-review → reviewing → integrating → done
```

`integrating` is on the path for work a Change Request owns — the repository
delta produced in a managed Story workspace. `accept_cr` is the only way in and
the integration transaction is the only way out; see step 4a below. Work done
inline, with no Change Request, has no `integrating` step and goes
`reviewing → done` on the reviewer's accepted verdict, with the commit
acknowledgement when `version_control.confirm` is on.

**Returns from reviewing:**
- `reviewing → analysis` — need more research
- `reviewing → to-dev` — code issues, needs rework

**Terminal from any status:**
- `any → closed` — won't do / cancelled
- `any → blocked` — concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision
- `blocked → backlog` (automatic) — the last live linked blocker reached `done`/`closed`; see *blocked vs is_blocked* below

**Note on closed:** Use `closed` for cancelled, obsolete, or won't-do items. Don't delete — close with a note explaining why. History is preserved. `handoff` refuses terminal elements; `set_status` refuses leaving `done` or `closed` with `TerminalStatusError` (`terminal_status`), naming the current terminal state. An identical `set_status` is idempotent.

For a leaf task or bug, `done` means its delivery was accepted and `closed`
means it was explicitly disposed without accepted delivery. A leaf may be
closed from any lifecycle state.

For a story or epic, `done` is computed completion: every direct child is
`done` or `closed`. `closed` is an explicit administrative disposition of the
container itself. A container may be closed only when every direct and
transitive descendant is already `done` or `closed`; an empty container also
satisfies this rule. Close or complete descendant work first, then close
containers from the inside out. `task-board validate` reports
`PARENT_STATUS_MISMATCH` for stored closed containers that still contain active
descendants.

Validation is strict by default for empty containers too: an empty Story/Epic
stored as `done` aggregates to `backlog` and is reported. A migrated local
board may explicitly set
`task-board.config.json -> validation.allow_legacy_empty_done_containers` to
`true` to suppress only that historical empty-`done` mismatch. Empty active
containers and every container with one or more children remain strict.

**Don't delete elements.** Use `closed` instead of `delete`. Deletion loses history and context. The only exception is removing test/junk data during board setup.

**Orchestrator review routing flow:** When a producer child sets `to-review`:
1. Orchestrator confirms the producer attached task-scoped outcome evidence.
2. Orchestrator spawns a reviewer child with explicit selection, for example `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`.
3. Reviewer child sets/keeps `reviewing`, reads artifacts/code, and writes the verdict.
4. If accepted → `done`. If changes are requested → `to-dev` (code rework) or `analysis` (needs more research), with notes/outcome evidence.
4a. When the reviewer was launched against a Change Request (a managed Story workspace run), acceptance is recorded by `accept_cr(ID, revision=N, evidence=...)` instead. That mutation writes `accepted` and `integrating` atomically: it sets the Change Request to `accepted`, stamps the reviewer run, binds the revision to its **producer role and archetype**, and routes the element to `integrating` — accepted but not landed, and accepted work never stays in `reviewing`. The Orchestrator routes a new tracked run carrying that exact producer role and archetype. Its model turn confirms preconditions and hands off fresh evidence; the runner then executes `task-board worktree checkpoint <ID>` for a **non-final** leaf or `worktree integrate` for the final leaf, synchronously under the integration lock. The model never starts or detaches those commands. A Change Request with `repository_delta=empty` creates no checkpoint commit and likewise stays `integrating`. `done` is produced only by the integration transaction, and a reviewer, orchestrator, or run whose role/archetype is not the bound producer's is refused. Runner landing refusals are typed terminal run events with the landing log attached and no autonomous successor. `accept_cr` accepts no `commit_ack` argument, its `evidence` must be a task-scoped outcome resource this reviewer run attached or updated (naming the producer's own `_results.md` is refused with `change_request_evidence_missing`), only the reviewer run that was handed the revision may call it (anything else is refused with `change_request_acceptance_unauthorized`), and the live task/reviewer checklist must be complete. All refusals precede CR, status, and activity writes.

4b. **`checkpoint` never closes anything.** A checkpoint makes no board transition at all: the leaf it commits stays `integrating`. When the leaf is the Story's last open child it is also its Story's integration unit, so `worktree checkpoint` refuses it with `change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint` and names `task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID>`, which lands the branch and is the only thing that makes the `done` transition. The refusal is a scope rule, not an acknowledgement rule: it applies whether or not `version_control` confirmation is enabled, and it leaves the branch, the accepted record and the board untouched so the integration path can still use them.

A checkpointed leaf is closed by its Story's integration, not by a later status change. `worktree integrate` lands the Story branch, which carries every checkpoint commit on it, so the same transaction that makes the final leaf `done` also closes every sibling that is `integrating` with durable checkpoint evidence — and only those. A sibling accepted but never checkpointed has nothing on the branch to land: it keeps blocking, and the fix is to checkpoint it, not to close it. That is also why a checkpointed sibling does not hide the Story's final leaf: the leaf that would close the scope is computed against the leaves this landing will carry.
5. On changes requested, the Orchestrator preserves the verdict, spawns the appropriate producer, and sends the next `to-review` handoff through a new reviewer. Repeat producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`.
6. If a child exits early or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, the Orchestrator inspects the run/outcomes and retries or reroutes through a focused child. Recoverable work is never handed to the human.
7. A `blocked` verdict stops routing only when it identifies a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision and records the full evidence packet; otherwise retry or reroute.

Recovery distinguishes transient failures from deterministic refusals. Provider
launch/transport failures retain the three-successor budget. A recorded
`handoff_unsatisfied` refusal is deterministic for the same brief and unchanged
tree: the runtime may queue one diagnostic successor, but an identical second
refusal becomes terminal `recovery_parked`, names `handoff_unsatisfied`, and
queues nothing. The refusal is recorded as non-recoverable even when that one
diagnostic successor is used.

### Stop-The-Line outcome packet

A producer that cannot hand off because of a concrete external blocker records
the boundary as the final section of a new or updated task-scoped outcome. The
packet grammar is Markdown headings in this exact order, with non-empty content
under every level-three heading:

```markdown
## Stop-The-Line
### Blocker
<the concrete external constraint>
### Evidence
<commands, exit codes, and observations proving the constraint>
### Options
<viable choices and their tradeoffs, including the recommendation>
### EXACT INPUT NEEDED
<the precise operator decision or external input required>
```

The runtime parses this structure; a prose mention of `EXACT INPUT NEEDED`, a
partial packet, an empty section, reordered headings, or a packet followed by
another level-one/two section is not a Stop-The-Line packet. At producer
completion, a valid fresh packet is classified `needs_operator` before
`handoff_unsatisfied`: the packet is persisted in element notes, the element is
set to `blocked`, the run records non-recoverable GoalRunSignal
`stop_the_line`, and no recovery successor is queued. Plain `spawn status` and
compact `spawn observe` expose that class as `blocked_by_external`. A producer
without the packet keeps the one-diagnostic-successor `handoff_unsatisfied`
path, and transient provider/runtime failures keep the three-successor budget.

The packet must live in an outcome of at most 1 MiB. At completion every
task-scoped outcome is hashed in full and an unchanged one is skipped before any
size is considered; a fresh outcome above 1 MiB is an evidence archive, never a
note, and is not inspected. A large archive left by an earlier round therefore
neither fails the run nor hides a packet written beside it (issue #350).

`reviewing` is a real state, not a pass-through. A reviewer child must do actual review work between the producer handoff and `done`; the orchestrator routes and monitors that review instead of performing the verdict inline.

`reviewing` is never a verdict, including under the Change Request flow. A reviewer run that ends at `reviewing` with no accepted Change Request is cancelled and a successor reviewer is spawned — that is what distinguishes "the reviewer decided nothing" from "the reviewer decided", and `accept_cr` does not change it. The accepted branch is derived from the Change Request record, not from the `reviewing` status.

`to-review` therefore means one thing: a producer handoff awaiting review. An accepted Change Request is `integrating`, never `to-review`; `task-board worktree status` and the Change Request state say how far that integration has come.

A tracked spawned producer cannot use direct `set_status(..., status=to-review)`
for its assigned element. That workflow guardrail returns a typed remediation to
run `task-board handoff ELEMENT --role ROLE`, which verifies checklist and
outcome evidence. Untracked operator status administration is unchanged.

`to-review` is an internal routing trigger, never a handoff of routine review to the human. Human input is reserved for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision.

Human-facing wording follows the board state. `to-review` means "ready for review" or "handed off to review"; it is not "done", "complete", "finished", "final", or "готово". Say a task is done only after the board item is actually `done`.

## Per-Element Review Policy

Every board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with
`create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with
`set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` query
projection. New elements and legacy elements without the field default to
`required`.

- `required` keeps the producer -> reviewer -> rework/reviewer loop. Only an
  accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.
- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but selection should favor a cheaper suitable effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates.
- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff from `to-review`
  directly to `done` only after checking acceptance criteria, checklist,
  validation results, and outcome evidence. Missing evidence returns to the
  producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.

This policy changes reviewer routing, not the need for board-owned evidence, and
never introduces routine human review.

## blocked vs is_blocked

Two different concepts:

- **blocked** (status) — explicit stop-the-line status for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Notes or an outcome resource must record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact human decision or external input needed to resume. Recoverable failures and ordinary rework are not `blocked`.
- **is_blocked** (computed) — from `blocked-by` dependencies; if dependency is not `done`/`closed`, element is blocked

A dependency link is a **fact** and stays recorded after the blocker is done.
The `blocked` **status** is a **condition**, and the board lifts it:

- A `blocked` element with at least one recorded `blocked_by` link is released
  to `backlog` automatically, inside the same mutation, when its last live
  linked blocker reaches `done` or `closed` — a direct `set_status`, a
  `handoff`, or an aggregate container transition. The link stays; the
  mutation result carries a `released` side effect with `released_by`, and the
  element's activity event (`operation: dependency`) records
  `status: blocked → backlog` plus `released_by: [<blocker>]`.
- Several linked blockers release only when **all** are `done`/`closed`.
- A `blocked` element with **no** `blocked_by` link is the stop-the-line state
  described above: it is never auto-released and never refused.
- An element that was already `blocked` with every recorded blocker terminal
  before a mutation ran is **stale**, not released — no transition in that
  mutation can be named as its releaser. `task-board validate` reports it as
  the warning `STALE_BLOCKED` (same severity as `UNSUPPORTED CONTAINER LINK`),
  naming the element and its blockers. Resolve it with an explicit
  `set_status`. A link whose target is missing from the board is unknown, not
  satisfied: it neither releases nor warns. `unlink` never changes a status.
  A container that carries an (unsupported) `blocked_by` link is released like
  a leaf, and deleting a done blocker leaves its dependent linkless — the
  stop-the-line state above, never warned.

CLI display:
```
TASK-05: to-dev [BLOCKED by TASK-02]     ← computed from dependency
TASK-07: blocked                          ← explicit status
```

## Automatic Parent Status Aggregation

After a child status mutation, the CLI recomputes every ancestor from its direct
children. It is derived state: an unassigned story or epic may follow an
assigned child without implying ownership of the container.

- If every child is `done` or `closed`, a non-closed parent becomes `done`.
  An already explicit `closed` parent stays `closed`; terminal child updates do
  not silently normalize it to `done`.
- Otherwise the parent adopts the highest-priority active child status:
  `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, then `backlog`.
  `blocked` children do not select a parent status; a parent with only blocked
  children keeps its current manual status.
- The recomputation can promote, escalate, reopen, or demote a parent, then
  continues through all ancestors. A parent that is `blocked` explicitly is not
  recomputed.

```bash
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=development)'
# TASK-260205-abc123 → development
# STORY-260205-def456 → development (auto-escalated)
# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → development (auto-escalated)
```

For Task/Bug records created with the current format, the first transition into
`development` also requires an explicit estimated value. Set it first with
`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The refusal
is a typed estimate-required error, parallel to the unfinished-dependency start
refusal. Records whose legacy `progress.md` has no Estimate section are
grandfathered and are not retroactively blocked; an idempotent status write for
an element already in `development` is not treated as a new start.

### Commit confirmation for accepted work

This gate exists so the commit-owning mover lays out its own delivered scope
as atomic, project-perspective commits and honestly acknowledges that scope is
committed — it is not a human checkpoint. An Orchestrator (or other
commit-owning mover) must not stop autonomous work to ask the human how to
split commits, or whether to acknowledge, on every commit or close. The mover
decides its own commit decomposition — how many commits, per-story or
feature-clustered, ordering, buildability — and commits that scope itself,
backdated per the project's commit-time policy when one is configured. Human
authorization is the standing grant to commit work, not a per-commit
approval.

This section is the single source for that contract. Role bodies, the spawn
templates, and the router carry only the ownership statement and a link back
here; they must not restate the mechanics below.

Top-level `task-board.config.json -> version_control.confirm` defaults to
`false`. When it is `true`, `desired_commit_time` is required. Any mutation
that would move a Bug or Story to `done` must then include the exact mover-local
acknowledgement `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This covers a direct Bug/Story
transition and a Task/Bug terminal transition that automatically promotes its
parent Story.

```json
{
  "version_control": {
    "confirm": true,
    "desired_commit_time": "evening MSK"
  }
}
```

```bash
task-board m 'set_status(STORY-260205-def456, status=done, commit_ack=scope_committed)'
```

The error reports the transition-causing element, every enforced Bug/Story
transition, the owner message, and the configured desired time. The
acknowledgement covers only the mover's scope; foreign uncommitted work is
allowed and task-board performs no git clean-tree check. Never acknowledge
`commit_ack=scope_committed` when the mover's own scope is genuinely
uncommitted — commit it first, then acknowledge. Local evaluation and
status writes share one mutation lock. In remote mode an enforcing CLI first
requires the server's `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1` capability, then
the server evaluates the acknowledgement and writes the child/ancestor statuses
under one serialized mutation.

A reviewer-archetype spawned run must not supply `commit_ack`, and must not
escalate the commit-or-acknowledge decision to the human. It records acceptance
evidence and hands it to the producer side — never to the human; every `done`
transition belongs there.
Which producer depends on the flow. An element under a Change Request routes
through `accept_cr` to `integrating` and is closed by the integration
transaction of step 4a, which supplies the acknowledgement itself. An inline
element with no Change Request keeps this gate: the producer that owns the
committed scope commits it and then makes the acknowledged transition.

**Board-state close rule.** A Story ends with its board-state commit on trunk, delta or not. A Story that `integrate` or `complete` closed already has one (`Record <STORY-ID> board state`). Every other close — hand `set_status(done)`, research with no repository delta, a candidate that landed outside the control root, `close-landed`, `integrate_external` — owes `task-board board commit <STORY-ID>` as the next step, run by the orchestrator or operator from the control root on trunk. `task-board validate` reports `board_state_uncommitted` while a done element's lane paths are dirty; the commit, not the acknowledgement, clears it — `commit_ack=scope_committed` asserts the repository scope is on trunk and explicitly excludes the Story's own board delta.

`board commit` follows the same commit-time rule as `integrate`: with `version_control.confirm` on, `--commit-time` RFC3339 is required and sets both dates; otherwise the current time is used. A stated commit time is never a publication time.

Close-landed and colocated integrate end in a published phase: after the board-state commit they push the board chain; a refused push returns `board_publication_pending` with the landing intact — run `task-board board publish` to finish the publication — and a code landing whose story commit still needs its pull request reports `awaiting_hosted_merge` with the delivery steps instead of failing.

Board structure decides how often this gate fires on an unfinished parent; the
rule that keeps it honest lives with the other decomposition rules in
[planning workflow](planning-workflow.md).

Structural reactivation follows one deterministic rule: adding a descendant creates it in
`backlog`, and adding or changing any descendant to an aggregating active status
runs ordinary aggregation through every ancestor. A terminal descendant cannot
be reactivated with `set_status` or `handoff`; create new work instead. Any `done` or `closed`
container on that path reopens to the highest-priority active aggregate status;
explicit closure is not sticky once descendant work becomes active. The target
is the aggregate child status, not a fixed `development` state:

```bash
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260205-abc123, status=to-dev)'
# Precondition: TASK-260205-abc123 is nonterminal.
# TASK-260205-abc123 → to-dev
# STORY-260205-def456 → to-dev (auto-reopened)
# EPIC-260205-ghi789 → to-dev (auto-reopened)
```

Automatic container transitions do not require an assignee. Hard dependencies
still gate automatic changes to `development`, `to-review`, `reviewing`, and
`done`: if a container has an unfinished blocker, its derived status is left
unchanged and the child mutation succeeds. Direct manual transitions use the
same dependency gate; they additionally require an assignee for `development`
and `to-review` — the two transitions that assert a specific worker is doing or
has done the work. `to-dev` is deliberately exempt: it asserts that the leaf is
queued and owned by nobody, so requiring an owner in order to record that would
be self-contradictory and would leave an unassigned blocked leaf with no route
back onto the developer queue.

`blocked` remains the existing non-aggregating exception: blocked children do
not select a parent status. Closed ancestors remain closed rather than being
silently normalized to `done`, so validation reports every affected container
that still has a blocked descendant. Resolve or close that descendant work; a direct terminal-status exit is refused.
For historical post-landing regressions, see the proven-landing
[`restore-done` route](tracked-background-spawn.md#integration-run-termination-and-historical-status-recovery).
An accepted candidate that already reached the protected trunk without
the integration transaction running — a revision accepted by an older binary,
or a non-final leaf landed by its own pull request — is closed by `task-board
worktree close-landed <ELEMENT-ID> --reason "..."`. The tool verifies the
evidence itself and records the method by name: `tree_carried` (the recorded
`candidate_tree_oid` is the tree of exactly one ancestor of the freshly fetched
protected ref that postdates the record's base commit; `landed_tree_not_on_trunk`,
`landed_tree_ambiguous`, `landed_commit_predates_base` otherwise),
`patch_contained` (the digest-verified patch resource reverse-applies to the
tip in a temporary worktree; `landed_patch_digest_mismatch`), or `pr_attested`
(`--landed-by-pr N`, the platform's merge commit on trunk — or, after a history
rewrite, exactly one ancestor with the same tree AND subject line, recorded as
`twin_of=<platform OID>` — and the element named; `landed_pr_not_on_trunk`,
`landed_pr_does_not_name_element`). A foreign checkout is named with
`--landed-in <path> [--protected <ref>]`; the
same tree/patch proof closes the CR as terminal `foreign_landing` and records
the foreign repository, ref and authority OID. `--landed-by-mr N --repo
<group/project>` adds GitLab `mr_attested` through `glab`, requiring merged
state, sha ancestry, and exact element naming. Its durable ledger proof keeps
the repository, ref, merged sha, authority OID, MR number, URL, and
`attested=true`. `--superseded-by <id>` records
terminal `superseded` only when the successor exists and is accepted or done.
Both terminal states release sibling producers. A queued run repeats this gate
on its next scheduler pass; current board parentage is intersected with the CR's
publication scope, so moving the blocker out of the Story with `set_parent`
also releases that same run without cancel+respawn.

A `task_delta`
closes only that leaf; a `story_final` closes the Story as integration would. A
zero-delta candidate needs its base on the protected ref and names no commit
(`zero_delta_base_not_on_trunk`); a CR-less integrating element is admitted as
`legacy_pr_attested` with `--landed-by-pr N` (the same PR proof and typed
refusals, with no fallback), or without the flag as `legacy_patch_contained`
or `research_outcome_only` or refused
`landed_close_missing_evidence`; an element the route is not for is
`landed_close_not_applicable`, and an unreadable record or a self-contradicting
one is `landed_close_indeterminate`. `--dry-run` verifies and writes nothing.
See [the Change Request lifecycle](change-request-lifecycle.md#the-change-request-lifecycle-in-order),
item 9.

## Story Activation Reminders

An `agent_reminders` array is added to the JSON envelope of a production
mutation whenever that mutation's persisted changes include a Story
transitioning out of `backlog`. It is **additive advisory output**: it never
fetches Git, never mutates the board beyond what the mutation already did, and
never replaces the authoritative freshness gate documented in
[task-board config](task-board-config.md#trunk-discovery-authority-and-refusals) —
a reminder can render every authority field `null` and the mutation still
succeeds.

### Trigger

A change qualifies when all of the following hold:

- `element_id` starts with `STORY-` (case-insensitive).
- `field` is `status` and the change's recorded `old_value` is exactly
  `backlog`.
- The new status is `analysis`, `to-dev`, or `development`. Every other
  target status — `to-review`, `reviewing`, `blocked`, `done`, `closed`, or a
  `backlog → backlog` no-op — does not qualify, and neither does a
  reactivation from `done` or `closed` (only `backlog` is the qualifying old
  value).

Both the mutation's primary change and every side effect are scanned, so a
Task/Bug mutation that auto-escalates its containing Story from `backlog`
still emits the Story's reminder even though the Task/Bug itself never
qualifies on its own ID prefix.

### Deduplication and order

Within one mutation's full change list (primary change first, then side
effects in their recorded order), only the first qualifying change per Story
ID produces a reminder — a batch that touches the same Story's status twice
(for example a reopen side effect followed by the requested transition) still
emits exactly one reminder for it. Distinct qualifying Story IDs each get
their own reminder, in first-seen order.

### Payload shape

```json
{
  "code": "story_worktree_base_preflight_required",
  "timestamp": "2026-08-29T12:34:56.123456789Z",
  "message": "Resolve and refresh the configured integration branch upstream, compare exact OIDs, and record the selected Story worktree base before provisioning or trusting it.",
  "story_id": "STORY-260218-bbbbbb",
  "trunk_ref": "refs/heads/main",
  "trunk_oid": "8c3f1a9...",
  "upstream_ref": "refs/remotes/protected/main",
  "upstream_oid": "5e2b7d0...",
  "selected_base_oid": null
}
```

`code`, `message`, and the RFC3339Nano UTC `timestamp` are stable across every
reminder in one mutation (one clock read per mutation, not per Story).
`trunk_ref`, `trunk_oid`, `upstream_ref`, `upstream_oid`, and
`selected_base_oid` are always present as JSON keys and are explicit `null`,
never omitted, whenever that specific piece of evidence is not known. The
`agent_reminders` key itself is only added to the result when at least one
change qualified; a mutation with no qualifying activation adds no key at all
(not an empty array).

### Authority evidence: local versus remote

The five nullable fields come from a cache-only projection that is read after
the mutation persists and never fails the mutation on error:

- **Local CLI** — prefers a persisted workspace freshness record from a prior
  `task-board spawn` for that Story (`trunk_ref`/`trunk_oid` = the recorded
  integration branch and its local OID at spawn time, `upstream_ref`/
  `upstream_oid` = the recorded upstream ref/OID, `selected_base_oid` = the
  OID the workspace actually branched from). When no such record exists, it
  falls back to a cache-only Git discovery (`DiscoverCachedTrunk`) against the
  configured `integration_base_branch` — the same discovery order as the
  freshness gate, but it never fetches, so `upstream_oid` reflects whatever
  the last fetch already cached — and `selected_base_oid` is `null`, because
  a cache-only projection names no provisioned workspace. Any read failure
  (unreadable tracking config, unreadable ref) renders every field `null`
  rather than failing the mutation.
- **Remote (board-server)** — the reminder is produced by the identical shared
  `pkg/board/query` mutation code, so it fires on the same triggers with the
  same dedup, but the server wires no authority source (there is no local Git
  checkout to read), so every one of the five fields is always `null`; only
  `code`, `timestamp`, `message`, and `story_id` are populated.

### Mutation coverage

`agent_reminders` is decorated by one shared boundary that a subset of
production mutations route their persisted changes through:

| Covered — can emit a reminder | Not wired — structurally cannot emit |
| --- | --- |
| `set_status` | `normalize_legacy_status` |
| `handoff` | `set_estimate` |
| `create` (only via its ancestor-reopen side effects) | `set_details`, `rename` |
| `delete` | `check_item`, `uncheck_item`, `add_checklist_item` |
| `set_parent` | `set_notes` |
| `assign`, `unassign`, `link`, `unlink` (wired, but their own changes are never `status` changes, so they only ever forward an ancestor side effect) | context mutations |

`assign`/`unassign`/`link`/`unlink` are listed on the covered side because
they share the same decorator plumbing as `set_status`; they simply never
produce a qualifying change themselves. The "not wired" column takes no `cfg`
at all in `registerMutations`, so it cannot mint a reminder even if a caller
crafted a matching change shape.

## Landing a Story on trunk

This section extends the commit-confirmation contract above to the one path
where a `done` transition is produced by a command rather than typed by hand:
`task-board worktree integrate`, which lands a Story developed in a managed
Story workspace. The mechanics of the managed workspace itself live in
[tracked background spawn](tracked-background-spawn.md#story-worktree-isolation);
what belongs here is the ordering and the exact scope of the acknowledgement.

**Accepted comes before done, always.** A reviewer records acceptance with
`accept_cr`, which routes the element to `integrating` and binds the revision to
its producer role and archetype. No Git operation has happened at that point and
nothing is `done`. The Orchestrator then routes a new tracked run with that
producer binding. Its model confirms preconditions and hands off; the runner
integrates in the foreground, and the `done` transition is the *last*
board effect of a run that has already moved trunk. The superseded order — close the
board first, land the work afterwards — is withdrawn, because it produced an
acknowledgement that attested a scope no repository had yet seen.

```bash
task-board worktree integrate STORY-260205-def456 \
  --cr TASK-260205-abc123 --revision 3 \
  --commit-time 2026-08-06T20:01:00+03:00
```

**The landing gate defaults to refusing.** `integrate` requires the
`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` suite to be configured; with no
suite it refuses `validation_not_configured` before any ref moves. A project
with nothing to run states that explicitly as `{"commands": []}`. See
[task-board config](task-board-config.md#the-landing-gate-suite).

**Squash, fast-forward-only, and one explicit time.** The accepted Story
candidate lands as one squash commit on trunk; the branch's internal Task
checkpoints are discarded rather than replayed, and trunk is only ever
fast-forwarded. When `version_control.confirm` is `true`, `--commit-time` is
required, must parse as RFC3339, and is applied to the author date and the
committer date of both commits this command creates. The example above is this
repository's own policy expressed concretely: the previous day at 20:01 MSK,
that is `2026-08-06T20:01:00+03:00` for an integration run on 2026-08-07. With
confirmation disabled and no flag, the current time is used instead.

**What the acknowledgement asserts here, and what it does not.** The command
supplies `commit_ack=scope_committed` on the `done` transition itself, and that
transition is not reachable until the Story's squash commit is genuinely
contained in trunk — a resumed run whose trunk no longer carries the recorded
candidate refuses rather than stamping the acknowledgement. So the assertion is
exactly: *the repository scope of this Story is on trunk.* It is narrower than
a clean tree in three specific ways, and each of them is a normal, expected
state at the moment the acknowledgement is written:

- **The Story's own board delta is still uncommitted.** The board-state commit
  is created *after* the transition, precisely because the transition is what
  produces the board bytes it records.
- **Board paths outside this Story are untouched.** The second commit stages an
  explicit pathspec: this Story's own subtree, the resource directories of its
  elements, and the ancestor aggregation files on its chain. Everything else
  under `.task-board/` — `.task-board/.goals/**` and every other Story's
  subtree — stays exactly as it was: ordinary uncommitted working-tree state
  that this command neither examines nor stages.
- **Foreign uncommitted work is untouched**, on the same rule the section above
  states for a hand-typed acknowledgement.

**The two commits, in order.** First the squash commit carrying the accepted
candidate tree; then the `done` transition; then a second, board-only commit
titled `Record <STORY-ID> board state`. A Story whose accepted candidate has no
repository delta produces no first commit and still produces the second one —
its board record is the whole of its work product.

**Recovery is re-running the same command.** Integration keeps one durable
transaction record per Story, so a crashed or interrupted run resumes the
recorded phase instead of starting a second integration, and a completed one is
a no-op rather than a duplicate landing. A rollback is offered in exactly one
phase — nothing landed yet, trunk unmoved — and is requested explicitly with
`--rollback`; no later phase undoes a commit that is already on trunk. Partial
integration is therefore a state to resume, never a state to clean up by hand.

### Ready Change Request recovery

Ordinary `set_status(done)` refuses an existing Change Request from **every**
source status, including `done`, with `change_request_state_conflict`. A failed
CR lookup returns `change_request_read_failed`; it never enables the inline
route. This applies to operator and spawned callers, apply and dry-run, including
`review=none` and `commit_ack`. Use review/checkpoint/integration normally.

For an already-landed **ready task_delta**, an operator can instead run:

```bash
task-board worktree close-landed TASK-260219-ghi789 --review-resource TASK-260219-ghi789_review.md --reason "repair already-landed revision"
```

The attached task-scoped outcome must contain exactly one fenced block:

````markdown
```landed-review
{"element_id":"TASK-260219-ghi789","revision":2,"candidate_tree_oid":"<full tree OID>","verdict":"accept","reviewer":"reviewer identity"}
```
````

`ready_tree_review_attested` verifies the exact candidate tree against freshly
resolved bound protected authority. At least one carrier must postdate the CR
base. Multiple eligible carriers are retained, sorted; `tree_proof_commit_oid`
is the first witness, not a claimed unique historical landing commit. No patch,
zero-delta, PR/MR, foreign-checkout or supersession fallback applies.

The method records integrated, authority ref/OID, witness list, review name and
SHA-256, reviewed revision/tree, reason and `attested=true`. This is an explicit
operator attestation of attached review, not normal reviewer-run acceptance.
Board status must be to-review, reviewing, integrating or done. Only the target
leaf closes. Done+ready is repaired; done+integrated retries revalidate the same
review binding without duplicating the ledger. Spawned apply refuses; spawned
`--dry-run` reports intended writes without persisting them. Unsupported CR
kinds/states and alternative proof flags return `landed_close_not_applicable`.
Missing or invalid review binding returns `landed_close_missing_evidence`; read
failure and evidence drift fail closed. Withdrawal is abandonment, not proof
that work landed.

